{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LtfmtfhzRTVh2LW9wq8RFa","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Reframed ''When Debiasing Hurts'' narrative fully lands. Reviewers\n2\t  (NLP/CSS-oriented, not deep measurement-error specialists) accept the bias identity\n3\t  as novel formalization in the LLM annotation context. Three-layer contribution structure\n4\t  — (1) exact bias identity, (2) EC-HTE as identity-prescribed correction, (3) Δπ×Δτ\n5\t  diagnostic — reads as coherent and complete. Honest disclosure of RMSE tradeoffs,\n6\t  K≥8 boundary, and real-CM negative results (exp-015/016) is perceived as mature\n7\t  scholarship, not weakness. Excitement 4.0-4.5 driven by ''debiasing can hurt'' surprise\n8\t  factor for NLP audience. Soundness 3.5-4.0 reflects minor concern about lack of\n9\t  convergence rates but acceptance of empirical thoroughness. No reviewer demands\n10\t  minimax bounds; ''problem identification + exact identity + diagnostic'' accepted\n11\t  as complete contribution package.\n12\t\n13\t  '\n14\timagined_experiments:\n15\t- name: HB EC-HTE Synthetic Validation\n16\t  plan_id: exp-001\n17\t  description: 'Hierarchical Bayesian Dirichlet-prior confusion matrix estimator with\n18\t    partial pooling. Full regime sweep: extreme/moderate/homogeneous × n_expert={100,250,500,1000,2000}\n19\t    × K={2,4}. 3600 MC runs total. MCMC diagnostics: PyMC NUTS, 4 chains, 1000 warmup.\n20\t\n21\t    '\n22\t  expected_results:\n23\t    mcmc_rhat_max: 1.004\n24\t    mcmc_ess_min: 1618\n25\t    mcmc_divergent: 0\n26\t    total_mc_runs: 3600\n27\t    runtime_min: 69\n28\t    hb_vs_mle_rmse_improvement_k2_n50: 4-9%\n29\t    hb_vs_mle_rmse_improvement_k4_n50_moderate: 6-9%\n30\t    hb_coverage_median: 0.93\n31\t    hb_coverage_min: 0.87\n32\t    hyperprior_sensitivity_k2_variation: <1%\n33\t    hyperprior_sensitivity_k4_variation: ~4-5%\n34\t    singularity_guard_triggers: extreme/n=50 only\n35\t  result_rationale: 'Best tier assumes reviewers accept HB partial pooling as well-motivated\n36\t    James-Stein shrinkage. R̂<1.01 and ESS>200 (actual: ESS>1618 with 4-chain fix)\n37\t    satisfy standard MCMC convergence criteria — NLP reviewers without deep Bayesian\n38\t    background will see these as reassuring checkboxes. 4-9% RMSE improvement over\n39\t    MLE at small n is modest but directionally correct; reviewers accept this as ''partial\n40\t    pooling helps when data is scarce'' (classical result, applied to new setting).\n41\t    Coverage median 0.93 (below nominal 0.95) is disclosed honestly — reviewer sees\n42\t    this as boundary condition acknowledgment, not flaw. Hyperprior sensitivity <5%\n43\t    for K=4 confirms robustness without requiring exhaustive sensitivity analysis\n44\t    — one table in appendix suffices.\n45\t\n46\t    '\n47\t  tables:\n48\t  - name: 'Table A1: MCMC Diagnostics Summary'\n49\t    columns:\n50\t    - Config\n51\t    - R-hat (max)\n52\t    - ESS (min)\n53\t    - Divergences\n54\t    - Runtime (s)\n55\t    rows:\n56\t    - - K=2/extreme/n=100\n57\t      - '1.003'\n58\t      - '1842'\n59\t      - '0'\n60\t      - '68'\n61\t    - - K=2/moderate/n=100\n62\t      - '1.002'\n63\t      - '2105'\n64\t      - '0'\n65\t      - '65'\n66\t    - - K=4/extreme/n=100\n67\t      - '1.004'\n68\t      - '1618'\n69\t      - '0'\n70\t      - '82'\n71\t    - - K=4/moderate/n=100\n72\t      - '1.003'\n73\t      - '1790'\n74\t      - '0'\n75\t      - '79'\n76\t    - - K=4/extreme/n=50 (guard)\n77\t      - '1.003'\n78\t      - '1650'\n79\t      - '0'\n80\t      - '85'\n81\t  - name: 'Table A2: HB vs MLE RMSE Comparison'\n82\t    columns:\n83\t    - Config\n84\t    - HB RMSE\n85\t    - MLE RMSE\n86\t    - Improvement (%)\n87\t    - HB Coverage\n88\t    rows:\n89\t    - - K=2/extreme/n=50\n90\t      - '0.228'\n91\t      - '0.248'\n92\t      - +8.1%\n93\t      - '0.91'\n94\t    - - K=2/extreme/n=100\n95\t      - '0.142'\n96\t      - '0.145'\n97\t      - +2.1%\n98\t      - '0.93'\n99\t    - - K=2/extreme/n=250\n100\t      - '0.091'\n101\t      - '0.093'\n102\t      - +2.2%\n103\t      - '0.94'\n104\t    - - K=4/moderate/n=50\n105\t      - '0.195'\n106\t      - '0.213'\n107\t      - +8.5%\n108\t      - '0.89'\n109\t    - - K=4/extreme/n=50\n110\t      - '0.310'\n111\t      - '0.382'\n112\t      - +18.8%\n113\t      - '0.87'\n114\t    - - K=4/extreme/n=100\n115\t      - '0.210'\n116\t      - '0.225'\n117\t      - +6.7%\n118\t      - '0.92'\n119\t  - name: 'Table A3: Hyperprior Sensitivity (Gamma scale)'\n120\t    columns:\n121\t    - Scale\n122\t    - K=2 RMSE\n123\t    - K=4 RMSE\n124\t    - Coverage\n125\t    rows:\n126\t    - - 0.5x\n127\t      - '0.091'\n128\t      - '0.208'\n129\t      - '0.93'\n130\t    - - 1.0x (base)\n131\t      - '0.091'\n132\t      - '0.210'\n133\t      - '0.93'\n134\t    - - 2.0x\n135\t      - '0.091'\n136\t      - '0.219'\n137\t      - '0.93'\n138\t  figures_description:\n139\t  - 'Appendix figure: HB vs MLE RMSE ratio across n_expert, colored by regime. Shows\n140\t    convergence at n≥500.'\n141\t  analysis: 'Reviewers in best tier see this as thorough validation infrastructure.\n142\t    The 4-chain MCMC fix (resolving initial K=4/extreme/n=50 blowup) is disclosed\n143\t    as methodological due diligence. Key narrative: HB provides meaningful but modest\n144\t    improvement over MLE — the contribution is NOT ''HB is dramatically better'' but\n145\t    rather ''stratified correction with principled shrinkage enables reliable per-subgroup\n146\t    CM estimation.'' This framing aligns with the paper''s positioning as problem-identification\n147\t    rather than method-innovation. Reviewer concern about computational cost (69 min\n148\t    for full sweep) is preempted by noting this is a one-time calibration, not per-prediction\n149\t    cost.\n150\t\n151\t    '\n152\t- name: Bias Identity Verification\n153\t  plan_id: exp-002\n154\t  description: 'Formal derivation and empirical verification of bias = f(Δπ, Δτ) identity.\n155\t    Systematic parameter grid: Δπ ∈ {0, 0.05, ..., 0.25} × Δτ ∈ {0, 0.1, ..., 0.5}\n156\t    × K ∈ {2,4}. ''Debiasing can hurt'' proposition with constructive two-subgroup\n157\t    example. 520 non-trivial configs.\n158\t\n159\t    '\n160\t  expected_results:\n161\t    r2_k2: 0.994\n162\t    r2_k4: 0.974\n163\t    tightness_ratio_k2_median: 1.003\n164\t    tightness_ratio_k4_median: 1.023\n165\t    debiasing_hurts_pct: 50\n166\t    debiasing_hurts_pct_detail: 260/520 configs (excluding Δτ=0)\n167\t    max_bias_ratio_k2: 2.86\n168\t    max_bias_ratio_k4: 2.41\n169\t    coverage_2se_k2: 0.951\n170\t    coverage_2se_k4: 0.94\n171\t    headline_median_amplification: 2-3x\n172\t    headline_max_amplification: 5.9x (K=4, CausalForest, from exp-011)\n173\t  result_rationale: 'This is THE core contribution for best tier. R²=0.994 (K=2) and\n174\t    0.974 (K=4) confirm the identity is exact at population level — finite-sample\n175\t    noise explains remaining variance, NOT bound looseness. Best-tier reviewers accept\n176\t    this framing because: (1) ''bias decomposition identity'' language (not ''bound'')\n177\t    is technically precise, (2) tightness ratio ~1.0 means MC estimate matches analytic\n178\t    prediction almost exactly, (3) the 50% ''debiasing hurts'' rate is striking —\n179\t    it means global correction is a coin flip in terms of whether it helps or hurts\n180\t    across the parameter space. The ''2-3x median, up to 5.9x'' headline is calibrated:\n181\t    median is robust (not cherry-picked extreme), 5.9x is from CausalForest (exp-011)\n182\t    which amplifies via nonlinear estimation. NLP reviewers unfamiliar with Fuller/Carroll\n183\t    see the Δπ×Δτ product form as novel; the explicit ''this is classical in spirit\n184\t    but new in this specific context'' framing preempts the one reviewer who might\n185\t    know measurement error literature. K=2 max 2.86× vs K=4 max 2.41× (DIM) is counter-intuitive\n186\t    and strengthens novelty — NOT monotonic in K, demonstrating the identity captures\n187\t    non-trivial structure.\n188\t\n189\t    '\n190\t  tables:\n191\t  - name: 'Table 1: Bias Amplification Under Global Correction (Main Paper)'\n192\t    columns:\n193\t    - K\n194\t    - Δπ\n195\t    - Δτ\n196\t    - Analytic Bias\n197\t    - MC Bias (SE)\n198\t    - Ratio MC/Analytic\n199\t    - Global Hurts?\n200\t    rows:\n201\t    - - '2'\n202\t      - '0.05'\n203\t      - '0.1'\n204\t      - '0.005'\n205\t      - 0.005 (0.001)\n206\t      - '1.00'\n207\t      - 'No'\n208\t    - - '2'\n209\t      - '0.10'\n210\t      - '0.2'\n211\t      - '0.020'\n212\t      - 0.020 (0.002)\n213\t      - '1.00'\n214\t      - 'Yes'\n215\t    - - '2'\n216\t      - '0.15'\n217\t      - '0.3'\n218\t      - '0.045'\n219\t      - 0.046 (0.003)\n220\t      - '1.02'\n221\t      - 'Yes'\n222\t    - - '2'\n223\t      - '0.20'\n224\t      - '0.5'\n225\t      - '0.100'\n226\t      - 0.101 (0.005)\n227\t      - '1.01'\n228\t      - 'Yes'\n229\t    - - '2'\n230\t      - '0.25'\n231\t      - '0.5'\n232\t      - '0.125'\n233\t      - 0.127 (0.006)\n234\t      - '1.02'\n235\t      - 'Yes'\n236\t    - - '4'\n237\t      - '0.10'\n238\t      - '0.2'\n239\t      - '0.020'\n240\t      - 0.021 (0.003)\n241\t      - '1.05'\n242\t      - 'Yes'\n243\t    - - '4'\n244\t      - '0.20'\n245\t      - '0.5'\n246\t      - '0.100'\n247\t      - 0.103 (0.008)\n248\t      - '1.03'\n249\t      - 'Yes'\n250\t  - name: 'Table 2: Debiasing-Hurts Summary Statistics'\n251\t    columns:\n252\t    - Estimator\n253\t    - K\n254\t    - Max Amplification\n255\t    - Median Amplification\n256\t    - Hurts Rate (%)\n257\t    rows:\n258\t    - - DIM\n259\t      - '2'\n260\t      - 2.86x\n261\t      - 2.1x\n262\t      - 50%\n263\t    - - DIM\n264\t      - '4'\n265\t      - 2.41x\n266\t      - 1.8x\n267\t      - 50%\n268\t    - - CausalForest\n269\t      - '2'\n270\t      - 3.2x\n271\t      - 2.3x\n272\t      - 50%\n273\t    - - CausalForest\n274\t      - '4'\n275\t      - 5.9x\n276\t      - 3.1x\n277\t      - 50%\n278\t  figures_description:\n279\t  - 'Figure 1 (Main): Bias amplification heatmap — Δπ (x-axis) vs Δτ (y-axis), color\n280\t    = amplification ratio. Clear product-structure gradient. White diagonal = amplification\n281\t    = 1.0 boundary.'\n282\t  - 'Figure 2 (Main): Constructive ''debiasing hurts'' example — two-subgroup case\n283\t    showing over-correction in low-error subgroup, under-correction in high-error\n284\t    subgroup. Schematic + MC verification overlay.'\n285\t  analysis: 'Best tier reviewers rate this 4.0-4.5 excitement because the ''debiasing\n286\t    can hurt'' framing is genuinely surprising to NLP audience. Most NLP papers assume\n287\t    correction monotonically improves estimates; showing 50% failure rate across parameter\n288\t    space is a ''stop and think'' result. The exact identity (not a bound) is clean\n289\t    enough to be stated as Theorem 1 in under 5 lines. R² > 0.97 across all K values\n290\t    makes the empirical verification convincing without requiring formal convergence\n291\t    rate proofs. One reviewer may note the classical measurement error parallel (Fuller\n292\t    1987 Sec 2.3) — the explicit citation and ''new in LLM context'' framing deflects\n293\t    this to a minor comment rather than a novelty objection.\n294\t\n295\t    '\n296\t- name: Semi-Synthetic CSS Benchmark\n297\t  plan_id: exp-003\n298\t  description: 'TweetEval sentiment (N=45K). Inject known CATE heterogeneity, annotate\n299\t    with GPT-4.1. K=4 subgroups (text features: has_hashtag × text_length). 100 MC\n300\t    runs with HB integration. Methods: oracle, naive, global_corrected, stratified_mle,\n301\t    hb_ec_hte.\n302\t\n303\t    '\n304\t  expected_results:\n305\t    n_subgroups: 4\n306\t    misclass_rates: S0=0.05, S1=0.10, S2=0.15, S3=0.25\n307\t    delta_pi: 0.2\n308\t    debiasing_hurts_confirmed: true\n309\t    mc_runs: 100\n310\t    hb_vs_mle_n100_S3_extreme: +60% RMSE improvement (HB dramatically better)\n311\t    hb_vs_mle_n100_S2: +5-8% RMSE improvement\n312\t    hb_vs_mle_n250_S3: +5-17% RMSE improvement\n313\t    hb_vs_mle_n500: converged (<1.6% difference)\n314\t    boundary_condition: n=50 with extreme heterogeneity in tiny subgroups (S3=12%)\n315\t      shows moderate degradation\n316\t    hb_n50_S3_degradation: -57% RMSE (HB worse but not catastrophic)\n317\t  result_rationale: 'Semi-synthetic design validates the bias identity in a realistic\n318\t    annotation pipeline: GPT-4.1 on TweetEval sentiment produces genuine subgroup-varying\n319\t    misclassification (Δπ=0.20), not synthetic noise injection. Best-tier reviewers\n320\t    accept this because: (1) the DGP is fully specified (outcome generation + treatment\n321\t    assignment + CATE injection), satisfying the ''semi-synthetic transparency'' requirement,\n322\t    (2) Δπ=0.20 matches the exp-012 pilot finding (Δπ=0.200 from GPT-4.1), confirming\n323\t    the heterogeneity is real, not assumed, (3) the +60% RMSE improvement at n=100/S3\n324\t    demonstrates HB''s value precisely where it matters most — the high-error minority\n325\t    subgroup. The n=50 degradation is disclosed honestly and positioned as a boundary\n326\t    condition, not a failure. NLP reviewers see ''real LLM + real corpus'' as strong\n327\t    validation even though CATEs are injected. The 12% minority subgroup size (S2,\n328\t    S3) maps to realistic CSS scenarios (e.g., minority demographic groups in survey\n329\t    data).\n330\t\n331\t    '\n332\t  tables:\n333\t  - name: 'Table 3: Semi-Synthetic Benchmark Results (Main Paper)'\n334\t    columns:\n335\t    - Method\n336\t    - Subgroup\n337\t    - n_expert\n338\t    - '|Bias|'\n339\t    - RMSE\n340\t    - Coverage\n341\t    rows:\n342\t    - - Oracle\n343\t      - All\n344\t      - '-'\n345\t      - '0.003'\n346\t      - '0.045'\n347\t      - '0.96'\n348\t    - - Naive\n349\t      - S3 (high-error)\n350\t      - '-'\n351\t      - '0.089'\n352\t      - '0.109'\n353\t      - '0.82'\n354\t    - - Global Corrected\n355\t      - S3 (high-error)\n356\t      - '-'\n357\t      - '0.142'\n358\t      - '0.165'\n359\t      - '0.78'\n360\t    - - Stratified MLE\n361\t      - S3 (high-error)\n362\t      - '100'\n363\t      - '0.038'\n364\t      - '0.195'\n365\t      - '0.91'\n366\t    - - HB EC-HTE\n367\t      - S3 (high-error)\n368\t      - '100'\n369\t      - '0.015'\n370\t      - '0.078'\n371\t      - '0.93'\n372\t    - - HB EC-HTE\n373\t      - S3 (high-error)\n374\t      - '250'\n375\t      - '0.008'\n376\t      - '0.062'\n377\t      - '0.94'\n378\t    - - HB EC-HTE\n379\t      - S3 (high-error)\n380\t      - '500'\n381\t      - '0.004'\n382\t      - '0.051'\n383\t      - '0.95'\n384\t    - - Global Corrected\n385\t      - S0 (low-error)\n386\t      - '-'\n387\t      - '0.067'\n388\t      - '0.082'\n389\t      - '0.85'\n390\t    - - HB EC-HTE\n391\t      - S0 (low-error)\n392\t      - '100'\n393\t      - '0.005'\n394\t      - '0.048'\n395\t      - '0.94'\n396\t  - name: 'Table A4: Debiasing Hurts in Semi-Synthetic Setting'\n397\t    columns:\n398\t    - Subgroup\n399\t    - Naive |Bias|\n400\t    - Global |Bias|\n401\t    - Amplification\n402\t    - Direction\n403\t    rows:\n404\t    - - S0 (π=0.05)\n405\t      - '0.012'\n406\t      - '0.067'\n407\t      - 5.6x\n408\t      - Over-corrected\n409\t    - - S1 (π=0.10)\n410\t      - '0.035'\n411\t      - '0.048'\n412\t      - 1.4x\n413\t      - Over-corrected\n414\t    - - S2 (π=0.15)\n415\t      - '0.058'\n416\t      - '0.052'\n417\t      - 0.9x\n418\t      - Slight improvement\n419\t    - - S3 (π=0.25)\n420\t      - '0.089'\n421\t      - '0.142'\n422\t      - 1.6x\n423\t      - Under-corrected\n424\t  figures_description:\n425\t  - 'Figure 3 (Main): Per-subgroup bias comparison — grouped bar chart, naive vs global\n426\t    vs EC-HTE, showing over/under-correction pattern in global, near-zero in EC-HTE.'\n427\t  analysis: 'The semi-synthetic benchmark is the paper''s empirical centerpiece. Best-tier\n428\t    reviewers see the ''global hurts S0 by 5.6×'' result as the most compelling instantiation\n429\t    of the bias identity — a low-error subgroup gets WORSE after correction, which\n430\t    is exactly the failure mode the paper identifies. The progression from n=50 (HB\n431\t    struggles) to n=100 (HB wins) to n=500 (converged) maps cleanly to the budget\n432\t    guidance in exp-006. Honest disclosure of n=50 boundary makes the n≥100 recommendation\n433\t    credible.\n434\t\n435\t    '\n436\t- name: Real-Data Case Study (Pattern Consistency)\n437\t  plan_id: exp-004\n438\t  description: 'UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech corpus. N=2496 comments. GPT-4.1\n439\t    annotation. Subgroups: platform_group × text_length (K=4). Pattern consistency\n440\t    analysis, NOT ground-truth CATE benchmark. Bootstrap CIs on point estimate differences.\n441\t\n442\t    '\n443\t  expected_results:\n444\t    dataset: UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech\n445\t    n_comments: 2496\n446\t    delta_pi: 0.117\n447\t    global_FPR: 0.139\n448\t    global_FNR: 0.533\n449\t    misclass_range: 0.266 - 0.383\n450\t    echte_vs_global_avg_bias: EC-HTE does not outperform global (high FNR limits correction)\n451\t    key_pattern: Δπ=0.117 confirms real-world heterogeneity exists\n452\t    honest_limitation: High FNR (53%) makes CM near-singular for positive class\n453\t  result_rationale: 'This is a negative/mixed result that STRENGTHENS the best-tier\n454\t    narrative. The real-data case shows: (1) misclassification heterogeneity exists\n455\t    in real LLM annotation (Δπ=0.117, statistically significant), validating the premise\n456\t    that global correction is mis-specified in practice, (2) EC-HTE does NOT outperform\n457\t    when FNR≈53% (near-random for positive class) — this connects to the honest boundary\n458\t    condition from exp-015/016 and the ''classifier quality threshold'' limitation.\n459\t    Best-tier reviewers see this as mature scholarship: the authors don''t cherry-pick\n460\t    favorable datasets, they show where the method works AND where it doesn''t, and\n461\t    explain WHY (near-singular CM). The ''pattern consistency, not ground-truth benchmark''\n462\t    framing is accepted because reviewers agree no ground-truth CATE exists for observational\n463\t    data.\n464\t\n465\t    '\n466\t  tables:\n467\t  - name: 'Table 4: Real-Data Misclassification Heterogeneity (Main Paper)'\n468\t    columns:\n469\t    - Subgroup\n470\t    - n\n471\t    - FPR\n472\t    - FNR\n473\t    - Overall Misclass Rate\n474\t    rows:\n475\t    - - plat0_long\n476\t      - '624'\n477\t      - '0.08'\n478\t      - '0.58'\n479\t      - '0.266'\n480\t    - - plat0_short\n481\t      - '624'\n482\t      - '0.12'\n483\t      - '0.62'\n484\t      - '0.337'\n485\t    - - plat23_long\n486\t      - '624'\n487\t      - '0.10'\n488\t      - '0.55'\n489\t      - '0.303'\n490\t    - - plat23_short\n491\t      - '624'\n492\t      - '0.15'\n493\t      - '0.59'\n494\t      - '0.383'\n495\t    - - Global (pooled)\n496\t      - '2496'\n497\t      - '0.139'\n498\t      - '0.533'\n499\t      - '0.322'\n500\t  - name: 'Table A5: Real-Data CATE Comparison'\n501\t    columns:\n502\t    - Method\n503\t    - Avg |Bias|\n504\t    - Bootstrap 95% CI Width\n505\t    - Note\n506\t    rows:\n507\t    - - Naive\n508\t      - '0.210'\n509\t      - '0.18'\n510\t      - Baseline\n511\t    - - Global Corrected\n512\t      - '0.273'\n513\t      - '0.22'\n514\t      - FNR=53% amplifies noise\n515\t    - - EC-HTE\n516\t      - '0.313'\n517\t      - '0.31'\n518\t      - Wide CIs due to near-singular CM\n519\t    - - Note\n520\t      - '-'\n521\t      - '-'\n522\t      - No ground-truth CATE; pattern comparison only\n523\t  figures_description:\n524\t  - 'Appendix figure: Per-subgroup misclassification rate bar chart with 95% bootstrap\n525\t    CIs, showing statistically significant heterogeneity.'\n526\t  analysis: 'Best-tier reviewers (70-85% accept EC-HTE as useful) interpret this correctly:\n527\t    the negative real-data result is a feature, not a bug. It demonstrates the diagnostic\n528\t    criterion''s value — a practitioner would compute Δπ=0.117 and FNR=0.533, conclude\n529\t    the classifier quality is insufficient for stratified correction, and stick with\n530\t    global correction or improve the classifier. This directly validates Claim 3 (diagnostic)\n531\t    by showing a real scenario where the diagnostic would correctly advise AGAINST\n532\t    EC-HTE. Reviewers note the honest framing approvingly: ''the authors show both\n533\t    success and failure cases.'' Revision request: one reviewer asks for a second\n534\t    real dataset with lower FNR to show EC-HTE working — this is classified as minor\n535\t    revision.\n536\t\n537\t    '\n538\t- name: Diagnostic Criterion Validation\n539\t  plan_id: exp-005\n540\t  description: 'Compute Δπ × Δτ product from pilot data across all experimental settings.\n541\t    ROC analysis of threshold for predicting when global correction fails. Cross-validated\n542\t    (NOT in-sample) AUC.\n543\t\n544\t    '\n545\t  expected_results:\n546\t    subgroup_level_auc: 0.833\n547\t    subgroup_correlation: 0.86\n548\t    setting_level_auc: 0.49\n549\t    low_pi_hurt_pct: 84.6\n550\t    high_pi_hurt_pct: 15.4\n551\t    theory_alignment: consistent with exp-002 formula\n552\t  result_rationale: 'Subgroup-level AUC=0.833 is strong for a zero-cost diagnostic.\n553\t    Best-tier reviewers accept this as ''useful practical tool'' (65-80% acceptance\n554\t    range). The setting-level AUC=0.49 (near random) is an honest disclosure — the\n555\t    diagnostic works at the subgroup level (predicting WHICH subgroups get hurt) but\n556\t    not at the setting level (predicting WHETHER any subgroup gets hurt). This is\n557\t    theoretically consistent: the bias identity is a per-subgroup statement (each\n558\t    subgroup has its own Δπ_k × Δτ_k), not a global statement. Reviewers see this\n559\t    distinction as demonstrating understanding of the theory''s scope. The 84.6% hurt\n560\t    rate for low-Δπ subgroups vs 15.4% for high-Δπ subgroups confirms the asymmetry\n561\t    predicted by the identity: low-error subgroups are over-corrected by global pooling.\n562\t    This is the ''debiasing hurts'' mechanism in action.\n563\t\n564\t    '\n565\t  tables:\n566\t  - name: 'Table 5: Diagnostic Criterion Performance (Main Paper)'\n567\t    columns:\n568\t    - Level\n569\t    - AUC\n570\t    - Correlation\n571\t    - Precision@80%Recall\n572\t    - Note\n573\t    rows:\n574\t    - - Subgroup-level\n575\t      - '0.833'\n576\t      - '0.86'\n577\t      - '0.79'\n578\t      - Primary diagnostic\n579\t    - - Setting-level\n580\t      - '0.49'\n581\t      - '0.12'\n582\t      - '0.51'\n583\t      - Near random (expected)\n584\t  - name: 'Table A6: Diagnostic by Subgroup Δπ Quartile'\n585\t    columns:\n586\t    - Δπ Quartile\n587\t    - Hurt Rate (%)\n588\t    - Mean Amplification\n589\t    - Diagnostic Recommendation\n590\t    rows:\n591\t    - - Q1 (lowest Δπ)\n592\t      - 84.6%\n593\t      - 2.8x\n594\t      - 'Flag: likely over-corrected'\n595\t    - - Q2\n596\t      - 62.3%\n597\t      - 1.6x\n598\t      - 'Caution: mixed'\n599\t    - - Q3\n600\t      - 38.1%\n601\t      - 0.9x\n602\t      - 'OK: near breakeven'\n603\t    - - Q4 (highest Δπ)\n604\t      - 15.4%\n605\t      - 0.5x\n606\t      - 'Safe: under-corrected, global helps'\n607\t  figures_description:\n608\t  - 'Figure 4 (Main): Diagnostic ROC curve at subgroup level with AUC=0.833. Annotated\n609\t    operating point at 80% recall.'\n610\t  - 'Appendix figure: Scatter plot of Δπ×Δτ product vs actual bias amplification,\n611\t    showing strong subgroup-level correlation (r=0.86).'\n612\t  analysis: 'Best-tier reviewers (65-80% acceptance for diagnostic) see AUC=0.833\n613\t    as ''good enough for practical use.'' The key selling point is zero cost — the\n614\t    diagnostic requires only a pilot confusion matrix, which practitioners already\n615\t    compute. Even without adopting EC-HTE, knowing when global correction backfires\n616\t    has standalone value. The setting-level AUC=0.49 is a limitation that reviewers\n617\t    note but don''t penalize heavily, because the subgroup-level diagnostic is what\n618\t    matters for the ''which subgroups to worry about'' decision. One reviewer may\n619\t    request cross-validation details — this is a minor revision request.\n620\t\n621\t    '\n622\t- name: Expert Label Budget Sensitivity\n623\t  plan_id: exp-006\n624\t  description: 'Systematic sweep: n_expert ∈ {50,100,250,500,1000,2000} × K ∈ {2,4}\n625\t    × regime ∈ {extreme,moderate}. Find RMSE crossover where EC-HTE beats global.\n626\t    288 total configs, 100 MC runs each.\n627\t\n628\t    '\n629\t  expected_results:\n630\t    total_configs: 288\n631\t    runtime_min: 110\n632\t    crossover_K2_extreme: 250\n633\t    crossover_K2_moderate: never (global always better on RMSE)\n634\t    crossover_K4_extreme: 1000\n635\t    crossover_K4_moderate: 2000\n636\t    hb_beats_mle_pct: 87.5% (21/24 configs)\n637\t    convergence_threshold: 'n≥1000: HB ≈ MLE (<0.5%)'\n638\t    best_hb_improvement: 'K=4/moderate/n=50/sgC: +42.4% RMSE over MLE'\n639\t  result_rationale: 'Budget sensitivity is critical for Claim 2 acceptance (70-85%\n640\t    in best tier). The crossover points provide actionable guidance: K=2/extreme at\n641\t    n=250 is practical (typical CSS study can afford 250 expert labels), K=4/extreme\n642\t    at n=1000 is feasible but expensive. The ''K=2/moderate never crosses'' result\n643\t    is an honest limitation that HELPS the narrative — it connects directly to Claim\n644\t    3 (diagnostic): when heterogeneity is moderate, the diagnostic correctly advises\n645\t    against EC-HTE. Best-tier reviewers see this as ''the method knows when to use\n646\t    itself.'' The 87.5% HB≥MLE rate confirms partial pooling rarely hurts (James-Stein\n647\t    guarantee in symmetric DGP). Convergence at n≥1000 shows HB advantage is most\n648\t    valuable in the data-scarce regime where it matters most.\n649\t\n650\t    '\n651\t  tables:\n652\t  - name: 'Table 6: Expert Budget Crossover Points (Main Paper)'\n653\t    columns:\n654\t    - K\n655\t    - Regime\n656\t    - Crossover n_expert\n657\t    - HB RMSE at Crossover\n658\t    - Global RMSE at Crossover\n659\t    rows:\n660\t    - - '2'\n661\t      - Extreme\n662\t      - '250'\n663\t      - '0.091'\n664\t      - '0.095'\n665\t    - - '2'\n666\t      - Moderate\n667\t      - Never\n668\t      - '-'\n669\t      - '-'\n670\t    - - '4'\n671\t      - Extreme\n672\t      - '1000'\n673\t      - '0.068'\n674\t      - '0.072'\n675\t    - - '4'\n676\t      - Moderate\n677\t      - '2000'\n678\t      - '0.055'\n679\t      - '0.058'\n680\t  - name: 'Table A7: Full Budget Sweep'\n681\t    columns:\n682\t    - n_expert\n683\t    - K\n684\t    - Regime\n685\t    - HB RMSE\n686\t    - MLE RMSE\n687\t    - Global RMSE\n688\t    - HB Wins?\n689\t    rows:\n690\t    - - '50'\n691\t      - '2'\n692\t      - extreme\n693\t      - '0.228'\n694\t      - '0.248'\n695\t      - '0.105'\n696\t      - vs MLE yes, vs Global no\n697\t    - - '100'\n698\t      - '2'\n699\t      - extreme\n700\t      - '0.142'\n701\t      - '0.145'\n702\t      - '0.098'\n703\t      - vs MLE yes, vs Global no\n704\t    - - '250'\n705\t      - '2'\n706\t      - extreme\n707\t      - '0.091'\n708\t      - '0.093'\n709\t      - '0.095'\n710\t      - Both\n711\t    - - '500'\n712\t      - '2'\n713\t      - extreme\n714\t      - '0.072'\n715\t      - '0.073'\n716\t      - '0.092'\n717\t      - Both\n718\t    - - '50'\n719\t      - '4'\n720\t      - extreme\n721\t      - '0.310'\n722\t      - '0.382'\n723\t      - '0.128'\n724\t      - vs MLE yes\n725\t    - - '250'\n726\t      - '4'\n727\t      - extreme\n728\t      - '0.135'\n729\t      - '0.140'\n730\t      - '0.110'\n731\t      - vs MLE yes\n732\t    - - '1000'\n733\t      - '4'\n734\t      - extreme\n735\t      - '0.068'\n736\t      - '0.069'\n737\t      - '0.072'\n738\t      - Both\n739\t  figures_description:\n740\t  - 'Figure 5 (Main): Budget crossover curves — RMSE (y-axis) vs n_expert (x-axis),\n741\t    separate lines for HB/MLE/Global, faceted by K×regime. Crossover points annotated.'\n742\t  analysis: 'Best-tier reviewers want actionable budget guidance. The ''Expert Budget\n743\t    Recommendation'' subsection distills this: ''For K=2 with high heterogeneity,\n744\t    250 expert labels suffice. For K=4, budget 1000. When diagnostic indicates moderate\n745\t    heterogeneity, global correction is the safer choice.'' This connects Claims 2\n746\t    and 3 into a coherent decision framework. The HB vs MLE comparison (87.5% HB wins)\n747\t    is a secondary but reassuring result — it justifies the Bayesian machinery over\n748\t    simpler alternatives.\n749\t\n750\t    '\n751\t- name: PPCI + DSL Baseline Comparison\n752\t  plan_id: exp-007\n753\t  description: 'Four DGPs: A (covariate-only error), B (outcome-only error), C (both\n754\t    errors), D (no error). 8 methods including EC-HTE, PPCI, EC-HTE+PPCI composition,\n755\t    DSL per-subgroup. 6912 total rows, 100 MC runs per config.\n756\t\n757\t    '\n758\t  expected_results:\n759\t    total_rows: 6912\n760\t    dgps:\n761\t    - A_covariate_only\n762\t    - B_outcome_only\n763\t    - C_both_errors\n764\t    - D_no_error\n765\t    methods: 8\n766\t    dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_ec_hte_ppci_bias: 0.005\n767\t    dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_ec_hte_bias: 0.178\n768\t    dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_ppci_bias: 0.004\n769\t    dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_naive_bias: 0.251\n770\t    bias_reduction_composition_vs_echte: 35x\n771\t    coverage_ec_hte_ppci: 1.0\n772\t    dgp_a_ec_hte_ppci_equals_ec_hte: true\n773\t  result_rationale: 'The PPCI composition result is the paper''s strongest empirical\n774\t    finding for best tier. EC-HTE+PPCI achieves near-zero bias (0.005) in DGP C —\n775\t    a 35× improvement over EC-HTE alone. This proves ''necessary and sufficient in\n776\t    composition'': EC-HTE handles structured covariate error, PPCI rectifier handles\n777\t    outcome error, composition handles both. Best-tier reviewers (NLP-oriented) see\n778\t    this as a complete pipeline solution: LLM annotation introduces BOTH error types,\n779\t    and the composition addresses both. The DGP A control (EC-HTE+PPCI = EC-HTE) confirms\n780\t    no double-correction — PPCI correctly contributes nothing when there''s no outcome\n781\t    error. DGP D (no error) confirms all methods reduce to oracle. The ''pure covariate-error''\n782\t    control (DGP A) demonstrates EC-HTE''s structural advantage: PPCI alone has higher\n783\t    variance because it treats covariate error as a generic black-box correction,\n784\t    while EC-HTE exploits the mixing matrix structure.\n785\t\n786\t    '\n787\t  tables:\n788\t  - name: 'Table 7: Method Comparison Across Error Types (Main Paper)'\n789\t    columns:\n790\t    - DGP\n791\t    - Method\n792\t    - '|Bias| (z0)'\n793\t    - RMSE (z0)\n794\t    - Coverage\n795\t    rows:\n796\t    - - A (cov only)\n797\t      - EC-HTE\n798\t      - '0.008'\n799\t      - '0.089'\n800\t      - '0.94'\n801\t    - - A (cov only)\n802\t      - PPCI\n803\t      - '0.045'\n804\t      - '0.132'\n805\t      - '0.88'\n806\t    - - A (cov only)\n807\t      - EC-HTE+PPCI\n808\t      - '0.008'\n809\t      - '0.089'\n810\t      - '0.94'\n811\t    - - B (out only)\n812\t      - EC-HTE\n813\t      - '0.085'\n814\t      - '0.112'\n815\t      - '0.87'\n816\t    - - B (out only)\n817\t      - PPCI\n818\t      - '0.006'\n819\t      - '0.098'\n820\t      - '0.95'\n821\t    - - B (out only)\n822\t      - EC-HTE+PPCI\n823\t      - '0.005'\n824\t      - '0.095'\n825\t      - '0.96'\n826\t    - - C (both)\n827\t      - Naive\n828\t      - '0.251'\n829\t      - '0.269'\n830\t      - '0.72'\n831\t    - - C (both)\n832\t      - EC-HTE\n833\t      - '0.178'\n834\t      - '0.226'\n835\t      - '0.83'\n836\t    - - C (both)\n837\t      - PPCI\n838\t      - '0.004'\n839\t      - '0.261'\n840\t      - '0.91'\n841\t    - - C (both)\n842\t      - EC-HTE+PPCI\n843\t      - '0.005'\n844\t      - '0.225'\n845\t      - '1.00'\n846\t    - - D (none)\n847\t      - All methods\n848\t      - ~0.002\n849\t      - ~0.045\n850\t      - ~0.95\n851\t  - name: 'Table A8: DSL Per-Subgroup Baseline'\n852\t    columns:\n853\t    - Config\n854\t    - DSL |Bias|\n855\t    - EC-HTE |Bias|\n856\t    - DSL RMSE\n857\t    - EC-HTE RMSE\n858\t    rows:\n859\t    - - K=2/extreme/n=250\n860\t      - '0.015'\n861\t      - '0.008'\n862\t      - '0.115'\n863\t      - '0.089'\n864\t    - - K=2/extreme/n=500\n865\t      - '0.010'\n866\t      - '0.005'\n867\t      - '0.085'\n868\t      - '0.072'\n869\t    - - K=4/extreme/n=500\n870\t      - '0.022'\n871\t      - '0.012'\n872\t      - '0.142'\n873\t      - '0.120'\n874\t  figures_description:\n875\t  - 'Appendix figure: 2×2 grid of DGP panels showing method performance. Highlights\n876\t    EC-HTE+PPCI dominance in DGP C.'\n877\t  analysis: 'Best-tier reviewers see the composition result as elevating the contribution\n878\t    from ''yet another correction method'' to ''principled framework for LLM annotation\n879\t    pipelines.'' The EC-HTE+PPCI composition addresses the realistic scenario where\n880\t    both covariate and outcome errors coexist — which is the default in LLM annotation.\n881\t    The DSL comparison shows EC-HTE''s structural advantage (mixing matrix exploitation\n882\t    + shrinkage) over generic per-subgroup correction. Reviewer concern about ''just\n883\t    use PPCI'' is preempted by DGP A results: PPCI alone has higher variance when\n884\t    error is purely covariate-side.\n885\t\n886\t    '\n887\t- name: Multiple LLM Annotator Comparison\n888\t  plan_id: exp-008\n889\t  description: 'Six OpenAI models: GPT-3.5-turbo, GPT-4-turbo, GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini,\n890\t    GPT-4.1, GPT-5.4. TweetEval sentiment. Measure per-subgroup misclassification\n891\t    heterogeneity across models. Test structural persistence of heterogeneity.\n892\t\n893\t    '\n894\t  expected_results:\n895\t    models_completed: 6\n896\t    all_delta_pi_above_005: true\n897\t    effective_delta_pi_range: 0.080 - 0.198\n898\t    worst_subgroup_consensus: True_medium (5/6 models)\n899\t    spearman_significant_pairs: 4\n900\t    gpt35_delta_pi: 0.198\n901\t    gpt41_delta_pi: 0.098\n902\t    gpt4o_delta_pi: 0.08\n903\t    gpt5x_delta_pi: 0.114\n904\t  result_rationale: 'This experiment validates the paper''s core premise: misclassification\n905\t    heterogeneity is STRUCTURAL, not model-specific. All 6 models across 3 generations\n906\t    (GPT-3.5 through GPT-5.4) show Δπ > 0.05 — the threshold where the bias identity\n907\t    predicts meaningful amplification. Best-tier reviewers see this as strong evidence\n908\t    that the problem won''t ''go away with better models.'' The worst-subgroup consensus\n909\t    (True_medium, 5/6 models) suggests text features driving misclassification are\n910\t    stable across architectures. The Spearman correlations (4 significant pairs) show\n911\t    error patterns are correlated across models — the same subgroups are hard for\n912\t    all LLMs. This is a P1 experiment that moves to appendix but provides crucial\n913\t    evidence for the ''this matters in practice'' argument. Best-tier reviewers cite\n914\t    this as supporting evidence when rating practical significance.\n915\t\n916\t    '\n917\t  tables:\n918\t  - name: 'Table A9: Cross-Model Misclassification Heterogeneity (Appendix)'\n919\t    columns:\n920\t    - Model\n921\t    - Overall Accuracy\n922\t    - Effective Δπ\n923\t    - Worst Subgroup\n924\t    - Best Subgroup\n925\t    rows:\n926\t    - - GPT-3.5-turbo\n927\t      - '0.78'\n928\t      - '0.198'\n929\t      - True_medium (0.39)\n930\t      - False_medium (0.10)\n931\t    - - GPT-4-turbo\n932\t      - '0.83'\n933\t      - '0.149'\n934\t      - True_medium (0.28)\n935\t      - False_medium (0.08)\n936\t    - - GPT-4o\n937\t      - '0.85'\n938\t      - '0.080'\n939\t      - True_medium (0.22)\n940\t      - False_medium (0.07)\n941\t    - - GPT-4o-mini\n942\t      - '0.82'\n943\t      - '0.136'\n944\t      - True_medium (0.30)\n945\t      - False_medium (0.09)\n946\t    - - GPT-4.1\n947\t      - '0.86'\n948\t      - '0.098'\n949\t      - True_medium (0.24)\n950\t      - False_short (0.07)\n951\t    - - GPT-5.4\n952\t      - '0.84'\n953\t      - '0.114'\n954\t      - True_medium (0.26)\n955\t      - False_medium (0.08)\n956\t  figures_description:\n957\t  - 'Appendix figure: Grouped bar chart of Δπ across 6 models, with horizontal line\n958\t    at Δπ=0.05 threshold. All bars above threshold.'\n959\t  analysis: 'Moved to appendix per paper framing guidelines. But best-tier reviewers\n960\t    reference it in reviews: ''The multi-model analysis convincingly demonstrates\n961\t    that heterogeneity is structural.'' The fact that even GPT-5.4 shows Δπ=0.114\n962\t    preempts the ''future models will fix this'' objection.\n963\t\n964\t    '\n965\t- name: Adaptive Expert Annotation Allocation\n966\t  plan_id: exp-009\n967\t  description: 'CATE-weighted allocation targeting high-CATE × high-error subgroups.\n968\t    Compare against uniform and uncertainty-only allocation. Stage 3 experiment —\n969\t    builds on exp-001 infrastructure and exp-006 budget analysis.\n970\t\n971\t    '\n972\t  expected_results:\n973\t    allocation_strategies:\n974\t    - uniform\n975\t    - uncertainty_only\n976\t    - cate_weighted\n977\t    uniform_rmse_k4_n500: 0.12\n978\t    uncertainty_rmse_k4_n500: 0.105\n979\t    cate_weighted_rmse_k4_n500: 0.095\n980\t    cate_weighted_improvement_vs_uniform: ~21%\n981\t    cate_weighted_improvement_vs_uncertainty: ~10%\n982\t    effective_crossover_reduction: Reduces K=4 crossover from n=1000 to ~700\n983\t    coverage_all_strategies: 0.92-0.95\n984\t  result_rationale: 'P2 experiment — appendix material. Under best tier, reviewers\n985\t    see adaptive allocation as a nice-to-have extension that demonstrates the framework''s\n986\t    practical flexibility. The 21% RMSE improvement over uniform allocation is meaningful\n987\t    but not dramatic — it confirms that allocation matters but doesn''t change the\n988\t    paper''s main conclusions. The ''reduces crossover from n=1000 to ~700'' result\n989\t    tightens the budget guidance from exp-006 but is incremental. Best-tier reviewers\n990\t    mention this favorably in ''additional contributions'' but don''t weight it heavily\n991\t    in their overall assessment. The CATE-weighted strategy''s advantage comes from\n992\t    concentrating expert labels where both error and effect heterogeneity are highest\n993\t    — this is a direct application of the Δπ×Δτ diagnostic (Claim 3) to allocation,\n994\t    creating a nice conceptual link.\n995\t\n996\t    '\n997\t  tables:\n998\t  - name: 'Table A10: Allocation Strategy Comparison (Appendix)'\n999\t    columns:\n1000\t    - Strategy\n1001\t    - K\n1002\t    - n_expert\n1003\t    - RMSE\n1004\t    - Coverage\n1005\t    - Crossover n\n1006\t    rows:\n1007\t    - - Uniform\n1008\t      - '4'\n1009\t      - '500'\n1010\t      - '0.120'\n1011\t      - '0.93'\n1012\t      - '1000'\n1013\t    - - Uncertainty-only\n1014\t      - '4'\n1015\t      - '500'\n1016\t      - '0.105'\n1017\t      - '0.93'\n1018\t      - '850'\n1019\t    - - CATE-weighted\n1020\t      - '4'\n1021\t      - '500'\n1022\t      - '0.095'\n1023\t      - '0.94'\n1024\t      - '700'\n1025\t    - - Uniform\n1026\t      - '2'\n1027\t      - '250'\n1028\t      - '0.091'\n1029\t      - '0.94'\n1030\t      - '250'\n1031\t    - - CATE-weighted\n1032\t      - '2'\n1033\t      - '250'\n1034\t      - '0.082'\n1035\t      - '0.94'\n1036\t      - '180'\n1037\t  figures_description:\n1038\t  - 'Appendix figure: Budget crossover curves under different allocation strategies,\n1039\t    showing leftward shift with CATE-weighted allocation.'\n1040\t  analysis: 'P2 experiment positioned as ''practical extension.'' Best-tier reviewers\n1041\t    note it approvingly but don''t consider it a core contribution. One reviewer may\n1042\t    suggest this as future work rather than appendix material — minor framing decision,\n1043\t    not a weakness.\n1044\t\n1045\t    '\n1046\t- name: Robustness to Misspecified Subgroup Structure\n1047\t  plan_id: exp-010\n1048\t  description: 'Test EC-HTE when subgroups are noisy/overlapping. Noise levels: 0%,\n1049\t    10%, 25%, 50% subgroup misassignment. K={2,4}. Coverage as decisive metric.\n1050\t\n1051\t    '\n1052\t  expected_results:\n1053\t    noise_levels:\n1054\t    - 0\n1055\t    - 0.1\n1056\t    - 0.25\n1057\t    - 0.5\n1058\t    total_runs: 800\n1059\t    k2_coverage_hb_range: 0.945 - 0.958\n1060\t    k4_coverage_hb_range: 0.946 - 0.960\n1061\t    k2_coverage_naive_range: 0.875 - 0.897\n1062\t    hb_beats_mle_all_configs: true\n1063\t    graceful_convergence_at_50pct: true\n1064\t    coverage_drop_0_to_50_noise: -1.3pp (K=2), -1.0pp (K=4)\n1065\t  result_rationale: 'Robustness is essential for reviewer acceptance of Claim 2 (70-85%\n1066\t    range). The key result is graceful degradation: EC-HTE coverage drops only ~1pp\n1067\t    from 0% to 50% noise, while maintaining nominal coverage (>94.5%) at all noise\n1068\t    levels. This directly addresses reviewer concern #4 (''how sensitive to subgroup\n1069\t    definition?'') with a quantitative answer: ''not very.'' The mechanism is HB partial\n1070\t    pooling — at 50% noise, subgroups are essentially random, and HB automatically\n1071\t    shrinks toward global (its prior), recovering global correction''s behavior. This\n1072\t    is the James-Stein guarantee in action: ''never much worse than the shrinkage\n1073\t    target.'' Naive coverage (87-90%) is consistently below nominal, confirming that\n1074\t    misclassification bias — not subgroup misspecification — is the dominant source\n1075\t    of coverage failure. Best-tier reviewers see this robustness analysis as demonstrating\n1076\t    methodological maturity.\n1077\t\n1078\t    '\n1079\t  tables:\n1080\t  - name: 'Table A11: Robustness to Subgroup Noise (Appendix)'\n1081\t    columns:\n1082\t    - Noise (%)\n1083\t    - K\n1084\t    - HB Coverage\n1085\t    - Global Coverage\n1086\t    - Naive Coverage\n1087\t    - HB RMSE\n1088\t    rows:\n1089\t    - - 0%\n1090\t      - '2'\n1091\t      - '0.958'\n1092\t      - '0.925'\n1093\t      - '0.875'\n1094\t      - '0.089'\n1095\t    - - 10%\n1096\t      - '2'\n1097\t      - '0.948'\n1098\t      - '0.935'\n1099\t      - '0.877'\n1100\t      - '0.092'\n1101\t    - - 25%\n1102\t      - '2'\n1103\t      - '0.950'\n1104\t      - '0.932'\n1105\t      - '0.890'\n1106\t      - '0.094'\n1107\t    - - 50%\n1108\t      - '2'\n1109\t      - '0.945'\n1110\t      - '0.945'\n1111\t      - '0.897'\n1112\t      - '0.096'\n1113\t    - - 0%\n1114\t      - '4'\n1115\t      - '0.956'\n1116\t      - '0.930'\n1117\t      - '0.904'\n1118\t      - '0.120'\n1119\t    - - 10%\n1120\t      - '4'\n1121\t      - '0.958'\n1122\t      - '0.953'\n1123\t      - '0.916'\n1124\t      - '0.122'\n1125\t    - - 25%\n1126\t      - '4'\n1127\t      - '0.960'\n1128\t      - '0.948'\n1129\t      - '0.909'\n1130\t      - '0.125'\n1131\t    - - 50%\n1132\t      - '4'\n1133\t      - '0.946'\n1134\t      - '0.944'\n1135\t      - '0.919'\n1136\t      - '0.128'\n1137\t  figures_description:\n1138\t  - 'Appendix figure: Coverage vs noise level, showing EC-HTE flat near 95% while\n1139\t    naive degrades. HB converges to global at 50% noise.'\n1140\t  analysis: 'Best-tier reviewers use this to rebut their own concern about subgroup\n1141\t    sensitivity. The ''automatic shrinkage to global at high noise'' property is intellectually\n1142\t    satisfying — it means EC-HTE is a strict generalization of global correction,\n1143\t    not a fragile alternative. Coverage as the decisive metric (not RMSE) is the right\n1144\t    framing: practitioners care about interval validity more than point estimate precision.\n1145\t\n1146\t    '\n1147\t- name: Causal Forest Integration\n1148\t  plan_id: exp-011\n1149\t  description: 'EC-HTE correction applied to CausalForestDML subgroup estimates. Compare\n1150\t    naive_forest, global_forest, ec_hte_forest. K={2,4} × regime × n_expert. 144 total\n1151\t    rows, 100 MC runs.\n1152\t\n1153\t    '\n1154\t  expected_results:\n1155\t    n_mc: 100\n1156\t    n_configs: 8\n1157\t    total_rows: 144\n1158\t    mean_abs_bias_naive_forest: 0.156\n1159\t    mean_abs_bias_global_forest: 0.107\n1160\t    mean_abs_bias_ec_hte_forest: 0.061\n1161\t    mean_rmse_naive_forest: 0.178\n1162\t    mean_rmse_global_forest: 0.166\n1163\t    mean_rmse_ec_hte_forest: 0.229\n1164\t    debiasing_hurts_max_ratio: 5.9x (K=4/extreme/sg0)\n1165\t    debiasing_hurts_cases: 9\n1166\t    k2_n500_ec_hte_bias_range: 0.004-0.013 (near zero)\n1167\t    pattern_consistent_with_dim: true\n1168\t  result_rationale: 'CausalForest integration is P0 because Claim 2 explicitly mentions\n1169\t    CausalForestDML — without this experiment, there''s a claim-evidence gap that\n1170\t    reviewers would flag as a killshot. The results show two key patterns: (1) EC-HTE\n1171\t    achieves 61% bias reduction vs naive and 43% vs global, confirming the correction\n1172\t    generalizes beyond DIM to nonlinear CATE estimators, (2) the ''debiasing hurts''\n1173\t    effect is AMPLIFIED with CausalForest (5.9× for K=4/extreme vs 2.41× for DIM)\n1174\t    — nonlinear estimation compounds the misclassification-induced bias. This amplification\n1175\t    is the headline ''5.9×'' number in the abstract. Best-tier reviewers accept the\n1176\t    RMSE tradeoff (EC-HTE RMSE 0.229 > global 0.166) because it''s disclosed honestly\n1177\t    and explained: variance-bias tradeoff from per-subgroup CM estimation. At n=500/K=2,\n1178\t    EC-HTE bias is near-zero (0.004-0.013) with competitive RMSE, showing the tradeoff\n1179\t    resolves with sufficient expert labels. The ''5.9× amplification with CausalForest''\n1180\t    result is particularly impactful for NLP audience — many CSS researchers use causal\n1181\t    forests, so this directly threatens their existing workflows.\n1182\t\n1183\t    '\n1184\t  tables:\n1185\t  - name: 'Table 8: CausalForest Integration Results (Main Paper)'\n1186\t    columns:\n1187\t    - Method\n1188\t    - Mean |Bias|\n1189\t    - Mean RMSE\n1190\t    - Max Amplification\n1191\t    - K=2/n=500 |Bias|\n1192\t    rows:\n1193\t    - - Naive Forest\n1194\t      - '0.156'\n1195\t      - '0.178'\n1196\t      - '-'\n1197\t      - '0.125'\n1198\t    - - Global Forest\n1199\t      - '0.107'\n1200\t      - '0.166'\n1201\t      - 5.9x (K=4/extreme/sg0)\n1202\t      - 0.033-0.109\n1203\t    - - EC-HTE Forest\n1204\t      - '0.061'\n1205\t      - '0.229'\n1206\t      - '-'\n1207\t      - 0.004-0.013\n1208\t  - name: 'Table 9: Debiasing-Hurts Cases in CausalForest (Main Paper)'\n1209\t    columns:\n1210\t    - Config\n1211\t    - Subgroup\n1212\t    - Naive |Bias|\n1213\t    - Global |Bias|\n1214\t    - Amplification\n1215\t    rows:\n1216\t    - - K=4/extreme\n1217\t      - sg0 (lowest π)\n1218\t      - '0.018'\n1219\t      - '0.106'\n1220\t      - 5.9x\n1221\t    - - K=4/extreme\n1222\t      - sg1\n1223\t      - '0.045'\n1224\t      - '0.078'\n1225\t      - 1.7x\n1226\t    - - K=2/extreme\n1227\t      - sg0 (lowest π)\n1228\t      - '0.022'\n1229\t      - '0.063'\n1230\t      - 2.9x\n1231\t    - - K=2/moderate\n1232\t      - sg0\n1233\t      - '0.015'\n1234\t      - '0.031'\n1235\t      - 2.1x\n1236\t  - name: 'Table A12: Bias-RMSE Tradeoff by Budget'\n1237\t    columns:\n1238\t    - n_expert\n1239\t    - K\n1240\t    - EC-HTE |Bias|\n1241\t    - EC-HTE RMSE\n1242\t    - Global |Bias|\n1243\t    - Global RMSE\n1244\t    rows:\n1245\t    - - '100'\n1246\t      - '2'\n1247\t      - '0.025'\n1248\t      - '0.185'\n1249\t      - '0.071'\n1250\t      - '0.112'\n1251\t    - - '250'\n1252\t      - '2'\n1253\t      - '0.012'\n1254\t      - '0.128'\n1255\t      - '0.065'\n1256\t      - '0.108'\n1257\t    - - '500'\n1258\t      - '2'\n1259\t      - '0.006'\n1260\t      - '0.095'\n1261\t      - '0.059'\n1262\t      - '0.105'\n1263\t    - - '250'\n1264\t      - '4'\n1265\t      - '0.032'\n1266\t      - '0.265'\n1267\t      - '0.095'\n1268\t      - '0.145'\n1269\t    - - '500'\n1270\t      - '4'\n1271\t      - '0.018'\n1272\t      - '0.195'\n1273\t      - '0.088'\n1274\t      - '0.140'\n1275\t  figures_description:\n1276\t  - 'Figure 6 (Main): Side-by-side comparison of DIM vs CausalForest debiasing-hurts\n1277\t    effect. Shows amplification increases with nonlinear estimator, especially at\n1278\t    K=4.'\n1279\t  analysis: Best-tier reviewers see CausalForest results as the strongest evidence\n1280\t    for practical relevance. The 5.9× amplification headline number comes from this\n1281\t    experiment and is the paper's most eye-catching finding. The RMSE tradeoff is\n1282\t    disclosed in a dedicated 'Bias-RMSE Tradeoff' subsection — reviewers appreciate\n1283\t    the transparency and note that at n≥500, the tradeoff largely resolves. The pattern\n1284\t    consistency between DIM and CausalForest ('same direction, amplified magnitude')\n1285\t    strengthens the theoretical claim that the bias identity captures a fundamental\n1286\t    mechanism, not an estimator-specific artifact. Combined with exp-017 (R-learner\n1287\t    replication), this establishes estimator-agnosticism as a validated property.\n1288\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"5a7fea04-88c6-4f40-a7d8-de32a19d47e7","uuid":"559135f7-203a-4d29-8834-eba30f2b5089","timestamp":"2026-05-23T20:10:28.860Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520/projects/ec_hte_llm_annotation/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260523T193110/best/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Reframed ''When Debiasing Hurts'' narrative fully lands. Reviewers\n  (NLP/CSS-oriented, not deep measurement-error specialists) accept the bias identity\n  as novel formalization in the LLM annotation context. Three-layer contribution structure\n  — (1) exact bias identity, (2) EC-HTE as identity-prescribed correction, (3) Δπ×Δτ\n  diagnostic — reads as coherent and complete. Honest disclosure of RMSE tradeoffs,\n  K≥8 boundary, and real-CM negative results (exp-015/016) is perceived as mature\n  scholarship, not weakness. Excitement 4.0-4.5 driven by ''debiasing can hurt'' surprise\n  factor for NLP audience. Soundness 3.5-4.0 reflects minor concern about lack of\n  convergence rates but acceptance of empirical thoroughness. No reviewer demands\n  minimax bounds; ''problem identification + exact identity + diagnostic'' accepted\n  as complete contribution package.\n\n  '\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: HB EC-HTE Synthetic Validation\n  plan_id: exp-001\n  description: 'Hierarchical Bayesian Dirichlet-prior confusion matrix estimator with\n    partial pooling. Full regime sweep: extreme/moderate/homogeneous × n_expert={100,250,500,1000,2000}\n    × K={2,4}. 3600 MC runs total. MCMC diagnostics: PyMC NUTS, 4 chains, 1000 warmup.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    mcmc_rhat_max: 1.004\n    mcmc_ess_min: 1618\n    mcmc_divergent: 0\n    total_mc_runs: 3600\n    runtime_min: 69\n    hb_vs_mle_rmse_improvement_k2_n50: 4-9%\n    hb_vs_mle_rmse_improvement_k4_n50_moderate: 6-9%\n    hb_coverage_median: 0.93\n    hb_coverage_min: 0.87\n    hyperprior_sensitivity_k2_variation: <1%\n    hyperprior_sensitivity_k4_variation: ~4-5%\n    singularity_guard_triggers: extreme/n=50 only\n  result_rationale: 'Best tier assumes reviewers accept HB partial pooling as well-motivated\n    James-Stein shrinkage. R̂<1.01 and ESS>200 (actual: ESS>1618 with 4-chain fix)\n    satisfy standard MCMC convergence criteria — NLP reviewers without deep Bayesian\n    background will see these as reassuring checkboxes. 4-9% RMSE improvement over\n    MLE at small n is modest but directionally correct; reviewers accept this as ''partial\n    pooling helps when data is scarce'' (classical result, applied to new setting).\n    Coverage median 0.93 (below nominal 0.95) is disclosed honestly — reviewer sees\n    this as boundary condition acknowledgment, not flaw. Hyperprior sensitivity <5%\n    for K=4 confirms robustness without requiring exhaustive sensitivity analysis\n    — one table in appendix suffices.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table A1: MCMC Diagnostics Summary'\n    columns:\n    - Config\n    - R-hat (max)\n    - ESS (min)\n    - Divergences\n    - Runtime (s)\n    rows:\n    - - K=2/extreme/n=100\n      - '1.003'\n      - '1842'\n      - '0'\n      - '68'\n    - - K=2/moderate/n=100\n      - '1.002'\n      - '2105'\n      - '0'\n      - '65'\n    - - K=4/extreme/n=100\n      - '1.004'\n      - '1618'\n      - '0'\n      - '82'\n    - - K=4/moderate/n=100\n      - '1.003'\n      - '1790'\n      - '0'\n      - '79'\n    - - K=4/extreme/n=50 (guard)\n      - '1.003'\n      - '1650'\n      - '0'\n      - '85'\n  - name: 'Table A2: HB vs MLE RMSE Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Config\n    - HB RMSE\n    - MLE RMSE\n    - Improvement (%)\n    - HB Coverage\n    rows:\n    - - K=2/extreme/n=50\n      - '0.228'\n      - '0.248'\n      - +8.1%\n      - '0.91'\n    - - K=2/extreme/n=100\n      - '0.142'\n      - '0.145'\n      - +2.1%\n      - '0.93'\n    - - K=2/extreme/n=250\n      - '0.091'\n      - '0.093'\n      - +2.2%\n      - '0.94'\n    - - K=4/moderate/n=50\n      - '0.195'\n      - '0.213'\n      - +8.5%\n      - '0.89'\n    - - K=4/extreme/n=50\n      - '0.310'\n      - '0.382'\n      - +18.8%\n      - '0.87'\n    - - K=4/extreme/n=100\n      - '0.210'\n      - '0.225'\n      - +6.7%\n      - '0.92'\n  - name: 'Table A3: Hyperprior Sensitivity (Gamma scale)'\n    columns:\n    - Scale\n    - K=2 RMSE\n    - K=4 RMSE\n    - Coverage\n    rows:\n    - - 0.5x\n      - '0.091'\n      - '0.208'\n      - '0.93'\n    - - 1.0x (base)\n      - '0.091'\n      - '0.210'\n      - '0.93'\n    - - 2.0x\n      - '0.091'\n      - '0.219'\n      - '0.93'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Appendix figure: HB vs MLE RMSE ratio across n_expert, colored by regime. Shows\n    convergence at n≥500.'\n  analysis: 'Reviewers in best tier see this as thorough validation infrastructure.\n    The 4-chain MCMC fix (resolving initial K=4/extreme/n=50 blowup) is disclosed\n    as methodological due diligence. Key narrative: HB provides meaningful but modest\n    improvement over MLE — the contribution is NOT ''HB is dramatically better'' but\n    rather ''stratified correction with principled shrinkage enables reliable per-subgroup\n    CM estimation.'' This framing aligns with the paper''s positioning as problem-identification\n    rather than method-innovation. Reviewer concern about computational cost (69 min\n    for full sweep) is preempted by noting this is a one-time calibration, not per-prediction\n    cost.\n\n    '\n- name: Bias Identity Verification\n  plan_id: exp-002\n  description: 'Formal derivation and empirical verification of bias = f(Δπ, Δτ) identity.\n    Systematic parameter grid: Δπ ∈ {0, 0.05, ..., 0.25} × Δτ ∈ {0, 0.1, ..., 0.5}\n    × K ∈ {2,4}. ''Debiasing can hurt'' proposition with constructive two-subgroup\n    example. 520 non-trivial configs.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    r2_k2: 0.994\n    r2_k4: 0.974\n    tightness_ratio_k2_median: 1.003\n    tightness_ratio_k4_median: 1.023\n    debiasing_hurts_pct: 50\n    debiasing_hurts_pct_detail: 260/520 configs (excluding Δτ=0)\n    max_bias_ratio_k2: 2.86\n    max_bias_ratio_k4: 2.41\n    coverage_2se_k2: 0.951\n    coverage_2se_k4: 0.94\n    headline_median_amplification: 2-3x\n    headline_max_amplification: 5.9x (K=4, CausalForest, from exp-011)\n  result_rationale: 'This is THE core contribution for best tier. R²=0.994 (K=2) and\n    0.974 (K=4) confirm the identity is exact at population level — finite-sample\n    noise explains remaining variance, NOT bound looseness. Best-tier reviewers accept\n    this framing because: (1) ''bias decomposition identity'' language (not ''bound'')\n    is technically precise, (2) tightness ratio ~1.0 means MC estimate matches analytic\n    prediction almost exactly, (3) the 50% ''debiasing hurts'' rate is striking —\n    it means global correction is a coin flip in terms of whether it helps or hurts\n    across the parameter space. The ''2-3x median, up to 5.9x'' headline is calibrated:\n    median is robust (not cherry-picked extreme), 5.9x is from CausalForest (exp-011)\n    which amplifies via nonlinear estimation. NLP reviewers unfamiliar with Fuller/Carroll\n    see the Δπ×Δτ product form as novel; the explicit ''this is classical in spirit\n    but new in this specific context'' framing preempts the one reviewer who might\n    know measurement error literature. K=2 max 2.86× vs K=4 max 2.41× (DIM) is counter-intuitive\n    and strengthens novelty — NOT monotonic in K, demonstrating the identity captures\n    non-trivial structure.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 1: Bias Amplification Under Global Correction (Main Paper)'\n    columns:\n    - K\n    - Δπ\n    - Δτ\n    - Analytic Bias\n    - MC Bias (SE)\n    - Ratio MC/Analytic\n    - Global Hurts?\n    rows:\n    - - '2'\n      - '0.05'\n      - '0.1'\n      - '0.005'\n      - 0.005 (0.001)\n      - '1.00'\n      - 'No'\n    - - '2'\n      - '0.10'\n      - '0.2'\n      - '0.020'\n      - 0.020 (0.002)\n      - '1.00'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - '2'\n      - '0.15'\n      - '0.3'\n      - '0.045'\n      - 0.046 (0.003)\n      - '1.02'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - '2'\n      - '0.20'\n      - '0.5'\n      - '0.100'\n      - 0.101 (0.005)\n      - '1.01'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - '2'\n      - '0.25'\n      - '0.5'\n      - '0.125'\n      - 0.127 (0.006)\n      - '1.02'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - '4'\n      - '0.10'\n      - '0.2'\n      - '0.020'\n      - 0.021 (0.003)\n      - '1.05'\n      - 'Yes'\n    - - '4'\n      - '0.20'\n      - '0.5'\n      - '0.100'\n      - 0.103 (0.008)\n      - '1.03'\n      - 'Yes'\n  - name: 'Table 2: Debiasing-Hurts Summary Statistics'\n    columns:\n    - Estimator\n    - K\n    - Max Amplification\n    - Median Amplification\n    - Hurts Rate (%)\n    rows:\n    - - DIM\n      - '2'\n      - 2.86x\n      - 2.1x\n      - 50%\n    - - DIM\n      - '4'\n      - 2.41x\n      - 1.8x\n      - 50%\n    - - CausalForest\n      - '2'\n      - 3.2x\n      - 2.3x\n      - 50%\n    - - CausalForest\n      - '4'\n      - 5.9x\n      - 3.1x\n      - 50%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 1 (Main): Bias amplification heatmap — Δπ (x-axis) vs Δτ (y-axis), color\n    = amplification ratio. Clear product-structure gradient. White diagonal = amplification\n    = 1.0 boundary.'\n  - 'Figure 2 (Main): Constructive ''debiasing hurts'' example — two-subgroup case\n    showing over-correction in low-error subgroup, under-correction in high-error\n    subgroup. Schematic + MC verification overlay.'\n  analysis: 'Best tier reviewers rate this 4.0-4.5 excitement because the ''debiasing\n    can hurt'' framing is genuinely surprising to NLP audience. Most NLP papers assume\n    correction monotonically improves estimates; showing 50% failure rate across parameter\n    space is a ''stop and think'' result. The exact identity (not a bound) is clean\n    enough to be stated as Theorem 1 in under 5 lines. R² > 0.97 across all K values\n    makes the empirical verification convincing without requiring formal convergence\n    rate proofs. One reviewer may note the classical measurement error parallel (Fuller\n    1987 Sec 2.3) — the explicit citation and ''new in LLM context'' framing deflects\n    this to a minor comment rather than a novelty objection.\n\n    '\n- name: Semi-Synthetic CSS Benchmark\n  plan_id: exp-003\n  description: 'TweetEval sentiment (N=45K). Inject known CATE heterogeneity, annotate\n    with GPT-4.1. K=4 subgroups (text features: has_hashtag × text_length). 100 MC\n    runs with HB integration. Methods: oracle, naive, global_corrected, stratified_mle,\n    hb_ec_hte.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_subgroups: 4\n    misclass_rates: S0=0.05, S1=0.10, S2=0.15, S3=0.25\n    delta_pi: 0.2\n    debiasing_hurts_confirmed: true\n    mc_runs: 100\n    hb_vs_mle_n100_S3_extreme: +60% RMSE improvement (HB dramatically better)\n    hb_vs_mle_n100_S2: +5-8% RMSE improvement\n    hb_vs_mle_n250_S3: +5-17% RMSE improvement\n    hb_vs_mle_n500: converged (<1.6% difference)\n    boundary_condition: n=50 with extreme heterogeneity in tiny subgroups (S3=12%)\n      shows moderate degradation\n    hb_n50_S3_degradation: -57% RMSE (HB worse but not catastrophic)\n  result_rationale: 'Semi-synthetic design validates the bias identity in a realistic\n    annotation pipeline: GPT-4.1 on TweetEval sentiment produces genuine subgroup-varying\n    misclassification (Δπ=0.20), not synthetic noise injection. Best-tier reviewers\n    accept this because: (1) the DGP is fully specified (outcome generation + treatment\n    assignment + CATE injection), satisfying the ''semi-synthetic transparency'' requirement,\n    (2) Δπ=0.20 matches the exp-012 pilot finding (Δπ=0.200 from GPT-4.1), confirming\n    the heterogeneity is real, not assumed, (3) the +60% RMSE improvement at n=100/S3\n    demonstrates HB''s value precisely where it matters most — the high-error minority\n    subgroup. The n=50 degradation is disclosed honestly and positioned as a boundary\n    condition, not a failure. NLP reviewers see ''real LLM + real corpus'' as strong\n    validation even though CATEs are injected. The 12% minority subgroup size (S2,\n    S3) maps to realistic CSS scenarios (e.g., minority demographic groups in survey\n    data).\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 3: Semi-Synthetic Benchmark Results (Main Paper)'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Subgroup\n    - n_expert\n    - '|Bias|'\n    - RMSE\n    - Coverage\n    rows:\n    - - Oracle\n      - All\n      - '-'\n      - '0.003'\n      - '0.045'\n      - '0.96'\n    - - Naive\n      - S3 (high-error)\n      - '-'\n      - '0.089'\n      - '0.109'\n      - '0.82'\n    - - Global Corrected\n      - S3 (high-error)\n      - '-'\n      - '0.142'\n      - '0.165'\n      - '0.78'\n    - - Stratified MLE\n      - S3 (high-error)\n      - '100'\n      - '0.038'\n      - '0.195'\n      - '0.91'\n    - - HB EC-HTE\n      - S3 (high-error)\n      - '100'\n      - '0.015'\n      - '0.078'\n      - '0.93'\n    - - HB EC-HTE\n      - S3 (high-error)\n      - '250'\n      - '0.008'\n      - '0.062'\n      - '0.94'\n    - - HB EC-HTE\n      - S3 (high-error)\n      - '500'\n      - '0.004'\n      - '0.051'\n      - '0.95'\n    - - Global Corrected\n      - S0 (low-error)\n      - '-'\n      - '0.067'\n      - '0.082'\n      - '0.85'\n    - - HB EC-HTE\n      - S0 (low-error)\n      - '100'\n      - '0.005'\n      - '0.048'\n      - '0.94'\n  - name: 'Table A4: Debiasing Hurts in Semi-Synthetic Setting'\n    columns:\n    - Subgroup\n    - Naive |Bias|\n    - Global |Bias|\n    - Amplification\n    - Direction\n    rows:\n    - - S0 (π=0.05)\n      - '0.012'\n      - '0.067'\n      - 5.6x\n      - Over-corrected\n    - - S1 (π=0.10)\n      - '0.035'\n      - '0.048'\n      - 1.4x\n      - Over-corrected\n    - - S2 (π=0.15)\n      - '0.058'\n      - '0.052'\n      - 0.9x\n      - Slight improvement\n    - - S3 (π=0.25)\n      - '0.089'\n      - '0.142'\n      - 1.6x\n      - Under-corrected\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 3 (Main): Per-subgroup bias comparison — grouped bar chart, naive vs global\n    vs EC-HTE, showing over/under-correction pattern in global, near-zero in EC-HTE.'\n  analysis: 'The semi-synthetic benchmark is the paper''s empirical centerpiece. Best-tier\n    reviewers see the ''global hurts S0 by 5.6×'' result as the most compelling instantiation\n    of the bias identity — a low-error subgroup gets WORSE after correction, which\n    is exactly the failure mode the paper identifies. The progression from n=50 (HB\n    struggles) to n=100 (HB wins) to n=500 (converged) maps cleanly to the budget\n    guidance in exp-006. Honest disclosure of n=50 boundary makes the n≥100 recommendation\n    credible.\n\n    '\n- name: Real-Data Case Study (Pattern Consistency)\n  plan_id: exp-004\n  description: 'UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech corpus. N=2496 comments. GPT-4.1\n    annotation. Subgroups: platform_group × text_length (K=4). Pattern consistency\n    analysis, NOT ground-truth CATE benchmark. Bootstrap CIs on point estimate differences.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    dataset: UC Berkeley Measuring Hate Speech\n    n_comments: 2496\n    delta_pi: 0.117\n    global_FPR: 0.139\n    global_FNR: 0.533\n    misclass_range: 0.266 - 0.383\n    echte_vs_global_avg_bias: EC-HTE does not outperform global (high FNR limits correction)\n    key_pattern: Δπ=0.117 confirms real-world heterogeneity exists\n    honest_limitation: High FNR (53%) makes CM near-singular for positive class\n  result_rationale: 'This is a negative/mixed result that STRENGTHENS the best-tier\n    narrative. The real-data case shows: (1) misclassification heterogeneity exists\n    in real LLM annotation (Δπ=0.117, statistically significant), validating the premise\n    that global correction is mis-specified in practice, (2) EC-HTE does NOT outperform\n    when FNR≈53% (near-random for positive class) — this connects to the honest boundary\n    condition from exp-015/016 and the ''classifier quality threshold'' limitation.\n    Best-tier reviewers see this as mature scholarship: the authors don''t cherry-pick\n    favorable datasets, they show where the method works AND where it doesn''t, and\n    explain WHY (near-singular CM). The ''pattern consistency, not ground-truth benchmark''\n    framing is accepted because reviewers agree no ground-truth CATE exists for observational\n    data.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 4: Real-Data Misclassification Heterogeneity (Main Paper)'\n    columns:\n    - Subgroup\n    - n\n    - FPR\n    - FNR\n    - Overall Misclass Rate\n    rows:\n    - - plat0_long\n      - '624'\n      - '0.08'\n      - '0.58'\n      - '0.266'\n    - - plat0_short\n      - '624'\n      - '0.12'\n      - '0.62'\n      - '0.337'\n    - - plat23_long\n      - '624'\n      - '0.10'\n      - '0.55'\n      - '0.303'\n    - - plat23_short\n      - '624'\n      - '0.15'\n      - '0.59'\n      - '0.383'\n    - - Global (pooled)\n      - '2496'\n      - '0.139'\n      - '0.533'\n      - '0.322'\n  - name: 'Table A5: Real-Data CATE Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Avg |Bias|\n    - Bootstrap 95% CI Width\n    - Note\n    rows:\n    - - Naive\n      - '0.210'\n      - '0.18'\n      - Baseline\n    - - Global Corrected\n      - '0.273'\n      - '0.22'\n      - FNR=53% amplifies noise\n    - - EC-HTE\n      - '0.313'\n      - '0.31'\n      - Wide CIs due to near-singular CM\n    - - Note\n      - '-'\n      - '-'\n      - No ground-truth CATE; pattern comparison only\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Appendix figure: Per-subgroup misclassification rate bar chart with 95% bootstrap\n    CIs, showing statistically significant heterogeneity.'\n  analysis: 'Best-tier reviewers (70-85% accept EC-HTE as useful) interpret this correctly:\n    the negative real-data result is a feature, not a bug. It demonstrates the diagnostic\n    criterion''s value — a practitioner would compute Δπ=0.117 and FNR=0.533, conclude\n    the classifier quality is insufficient for stratified correction, and stick with\n    global correction or improve the classifier. This directly validates Claim 3 (diagnostic)\n    by showing a real scenario where the diagnostic would correctly advise AGAINST\n    EC-HTE. Reviewers note the honest framing approvingly: ''the authors show both\n    success and failure cases.'' Revision request: one reviewer asks for a second\n    real dataset with lower FNR to show EC-HTE working — this is classified as minor\n    revision.\n\n    '\n- name: Diagnostic Criterion Validation\n  plan_id: exp-005\n  description: 'Compute Δπ × Δτ product from pilot data across all experimental settings.\n    ROC analysis of threshold for predicting when global correction fails. Cross-validated\n    (NOT in-sample) AUC.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    subgroup_level_auc: 0.833\n    subgroup_correlation: 0.86\n    setting_level_auc: 0.49\n    low_pi_hurt_pct: 84.6\n    high_pi_hurt_pct: 15.4\n    theory_alignment: consistent with exp-002 formula\n  result_rationale: 'Subgroup-level AUC=0.833 is strong for a zero-cost diagnostic.\n    Best-tier reviewers accept this as ''useful practical tool'' (65-80% acceptance\n    range). The setting-level AUC=0.49 (near random) is an honest disclosure — the\n    diagnostic works at the subgroup level (predicting WHICH subgroups get hurt) but\n    not at the setting level (predicting WHETHER any subgroup gets hurt). This is\n    theoretically consistent: the bias identity is a per-subgroup statement (each\n    subgroup has its own Δπ_k × Δτ_k), not a global statement. Reviewers see this\n    distinction as demonstrating understanding of the theory''s scope. The 84.6% hurt\n    rate for low-Δπ subgroups vs 15.4% for high-Δπ subgroups confirms the asymmetry\n    predicted by the identity: low-error subgroups are over-corrected by global pooling.\n    This is the ''debiasing hurts'' mechanism in action.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 5: Diagnostic Criterion Performance (Main Paper)'\n    columns:\n    - Level\n    - AUC\n    - Correlation\n    - Precision@80%Recall\n    - Note\n    rows:\n    - - Subgroup-level\n      - '0.833'\n      - '0.86'\n      - '0.79'\n      - Primary diagnostic\n    - - Setting-level\n      - '0.49'\n      - '0.12'\n      - '0.51'\n      - Near random (expected)\n  - name: 'Table A6: Diagnostic by Subgroup Δπ Quartile'\n    columns:\n    - Δπ Quartile\n    - Hurt Rate (%)\n    - Mean Amplification\n    - Diagnostic Recommendation\n    rows:\n    - - Q1 (lowest Δπ)\n      - 84.6%\n      - 2.8x\n      - 'Flag: likely over-corrected'\n    - - Q2\n      - 62.3%\n      - 1.6x\n      - 'Caution: mixed'\n    - - Q3\n      - 38.1%\n      - 0.9x\n      - 'OK: near breakeven'\n    - - Q4 (highest Δπ)\n      - 15.4%\n      - 0.5x\n      - 'Safe: under-corrected, global helps'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 4 (Main): Diagnostic ROC curve at subgroup level with AUC=0.833. Annotated\n    operating point at 80% recall.'\n  - 'Appendix figure: Scatter plot of Δπ×Δτ product vs actual bias amplification,\n    showing strong subgroup-level correlation (r=0.86).'\n  analysis: 'Best-tier reviewers (65-80% acceptance for diagnostic) see AUC=0.833\n    as ''good enough for practical use.'' The key selling point is zero cost — the\n    diagnostic requires only a pilot confusion matrix, which practitioners already\n    compute. Even without adopting EC-HTE, knowing when global correction backfires\n    has standalone value. The setting-level AUC=0.49 is a limitation that reviewers\n    note but don''t penalize heavily, because the subgroup-level diagnostic is what\n    matters for the ''which subgroups to worry about'' decision. One reviewer may\n    request cross-validation details — this is a minor revision request.\n\n    '\n- name: Expert Label Budget Sensitivity\n  plan_id: exp-006\n  description: 'Systematic sweep: n_expert ∈ {50,100,250,500,1000,2000} × K ∈ {2,4}\n    × regime ∈ {extreme,moderate}. Find RMSE crossover where EC-HTE beats global.\n    288 total configs, 100 MC runs each.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_configs: 288\n    runtime_min: 110\n    crossover_K2_extreme: 250\n    crossover_K2_moderate: never (global always better on RMSE)\n    crossover_K4_extreme: 1000\n    crossover_K4_moderate: 2000\n    hb_beats_mle_pct: 87.5% (21/24 configs)\n    convergence_threshold: 'n≥1000: HB ≈ MLE (<0.5%)'\n    best_hb_improvement: 'K=4/moderate/n=50/sgC: +42.4% RMSE over MLE'\n  result_rationale: 'Budget sensitivity is critical for Claim 2 acceptance (70-85%\n    in best tier). The crossover points provide actionable guidance: K=2/extreme at\n    n=250 is practical (typical CSS study can afford 250 expert labels), K=4/extreme\n    at n=1000 is feasible but expensive. The ''K=2/moderate never crosses'' result\n    is an honest limitation that HELPS the narrative — it connects directly to Claim\n    3 (diagnostic): when heterogeneity is moderate, the diagnostic correctly advises\n    against EC-HTE. Best-tier reviewers see this as ''the method knows when to use\n    itself.'' The 87.5% HB≥MLE rate confirms partial pooling rarely hurts (James-Stein\n    guarantee in symmetric DGP). Convergence at n≥1000 shows HB advantage is most\n    valuable in the data-scarce regime where it matters most.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 6: Expert Budget Crossover Points (Main Paper)'\n    columns:\n    - K\n    - Regime\n    - Crossover n_expert\n    - HB RMSE at Crossover\n    - Global RMSE at Crossover\n    rows:\n    - - '2'\n      - Extreme\n      - '250'\n      - '0.091'\n      - '0.095'\n    - - '2'\n      - Moderate\n      - Never\n      - '-'\n      - '-'\n    - - '4'\n      - Extreme\n      - '1000'\n      - '0.068'\n      - '0.072'\n    - - '4'\n      - Moderate\n      - '2000'\n      - '0.055'\n      - '0.058'\n  - name: 'Table A7: Full Budget Sweep'\n    columns:\n    - n_expert\n    - K\n    - Regime\n    - HB RMSE\n    - MLE RMSE\n    - Global RMSE\n    - HB Wins?\n    rows:\n    - - '50'\n      - '2'\n      - extreme\n      - '0.228'\n      - '0.248'\n      - '0.105'\n      - vs MLE yes, vs Global no\n    - - '100'\n      - '2'\n      - extreme\n      - '0.142'\n      - '0.145'\n      - '0.098'\n      - vs MLE yes, vs Global no\n    - - '250'\n      - '2'\n      - extreme\n      - '0.091'\n      - '0.093'\n      - '0.095'\n      - Both\n    - - '500'\n      - '2'\n      - extreme\n      - '0.072'\n      - '0.073'\n      - '0.092'\n      - Both\n    - - '50'\n      - '4'\n      - extreme\n      - '0.310'\n      - '0.382'\n      - '0.128'\n      - vs MLE yes\n    - - '250'\n      - '4'\n      - extreme\n      - '0.135'\n      - '0.140'\n      - '0.110'\n      - vs MLE yes\n    - - '1000'\n      - '4'\n      - extreme\n      - '0.068'\n      - '0.069'\n      - '0.072'\n      - Both\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 5 (Main): Budget crossover curves — RMSE (y-axis) vs n_expert (x-axis),\n    separate lines for HB/MLE/Global, faceted by K×regime. Crossover points annotated.'\n  analysis: 'Best-tier reviewers want actionable budget guidance. The ''Expert Budget\n    Recommendation'' subsection distills this: ''For K=2 with high heterogeneity,\n    250 expert labels suffice. For K=4, budget 1000. When diagnostic indicates moderate\n    heterogeneity, global correction is the safer choice.'' This connects Claims 2\n    and 3 into a coherent decision framework. The HB vs MLE comparison (87.5% HB wins)\n    is a secondary but reassuring result — it justifies the Bayesian machinery over\n    simpler alternatives.\n\n    '\n- name: PPCI + DSL Baseline Comparison\n  plan_id: exp-007\n  description: 'Four DGPs: A (covariate-only error), B (outcome-only error), C (both\n    errors), D (no error). 8 methods including EC-HTE, PPCI, EC-HTE+PPCI composition,\n    DSL per-subgroup. 6912 total rows, 100 MC runs per config.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    total_rows: 6912\n    dgps:\n    - A_covariate_only\n    - B_outcome_only\n    - C_both_errors\n    - D_no_error\n    methods: 8\n    dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_ec_hte_ppci_bias: 0.005\n    dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_ec_hte_bias: 0.178\n    dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_ppci_bias: 0.004\n    dgp_c_extreme_n250_k2_naive_bias: 0.251\n    bias_reduction_composition_vs_echte: 35x\n    coverage_ec_hte_ppci: 1.0\n    dgp_a_ec_hte_ppci_equals_ec_hte: true\n  result_rationale: 'The PPCI composition result is the paper''s strongest empirical\n    finding for best tier. EC-HTE+PPCI achieves near-zero bias (0.005) in DGP C —\n    a 35× improvement over EC-HTE alone. This proves ''necessary and sufficient in\n    composition'': EC-HTE handles structured covariate error, PPCI rectifier handles\n    outcome error, composition handles both. Best-tier reviewers (NLP-oriented) see\n    this as a complete pipeline solution: LLM annotation introduces BOTH error types,\n    and the composition addresses both. The DGP A control (EC-HTE+PPCI = EC-HTE) confirms\n    no double-correction — PPCI correctly contributes nothing when there''s no outcome\n    error. DGP D (no error) confirms all methods reduce to oracle. The ''pure covariate-error''\n    control (DGP A) demonstrates EC-HTE''s structural advantage: PPCI alone has higher\n    variance because it treats covariate error as a generic black-box correction,\n    while EC-HTE exploits the mixing matrix structure.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 7: Method Comparison Across Error Types (Main Paper)'\n    columns:\n    - DGP\n    - Method\n    - '|Bias| (z0)'\n    - RMSE (z0)\n    - Coverage\n    rows:\n    - - A (cov only)\n      - EC-HTE\n      - '0.008'\n      - '0.089'\n      - '0.94'\n    - - A (cov only)\n      - PPCI\n      - '0.045'\n      - '0.132'\n      - '0.88'\n    - - A (cov only)\n      - EC-HTE+PPCI\n      - '0.008'\n      - '0.089'\n      - '0.94'\n    - - B (out only)\n      - EC-HTE\n      - '0.085'\n      - '0.112'\n      - '0.87'\n    - - B (out only)\n      - PPCI\n      - '0.006'\n      - '0.098'\n      - '0.95'\n    - - B (out only)\n      - EC-HTE+PPCI\n      - '0.005'\n      - '0.095'\n      - '0.96'\n    - - C (both)\n      - Naive\n      - '0.251'\n      - '0.269'\n      - '0.72'\n    - - C (both)\n      - EC-HTE\n      - '0.178'\n      - '0.226'\n      - '0.83'\n    - - C (both)\n      - PPCI\n      - '0.004'\n      - '0.261'\n      - '0.91'\n    - - C (both)\n      - EC-HTE+PPCI\n      - '0.005'\n      - '0.225'\n      - '1.00'\n    - - D (none)\n      - All methods\n      - ~0.002\n      - ~0.045\n      - ~0.95\n  - name: 'Table A8: DSL Per-Subgroup Baseline'\n    columns:\n    - Config\n    - DSL |Bias|\n    - EC-HTE |Bias|\n    - DSL RMSE\n    - EC-HTE RMSE\n    rows:\n    - - K=2/extreme/n=250\n      - '0.015'\n      - '0.008'\n      - '0.115'\n      - '0.089'\n    - - K=2/extreme/n=500\n      - '0.010'\n      - '0.005'\n      - '0.085'\n      - '0.072'\n    - - K=4/extreme/n=500\n      - '0.022'\n      - '0.012'\n      - '0.142'\n      - '0.120'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Appendix figure: 2×2 grid of DGP panels showing method performance. Highlights\n    EC-HTE+PPCI dominance in DGP C.'\n  analysis: 'Best-tier reviewers see the composition result as elevating the contribution\n    from ''yet another correction method'' to ''principled framework for LLM annotation\n    pipelines.'' The EC-HTE+PPCI composition addresses the realistic scenario where\n    both covariate and outcome errors coexist — which is the default in LLM annotation.\n    The DSL comparison shows EC-HTE''s structural advantage (mixing matrix exploitation\n    + shrinkage) over generic per-subgroup correction. Reviewer concern about ''just\n    use PPCI'' is preempted by DGP A results: PPCI alone has higher variance when\n    error is purely covariate-side.\n\n    '\n- name: Multiple LLM Annotator Comparison\n  plan_id: exp-008\n  description: 'Six OpenAI models: GPT-3.5-turbo, GPT-4-turbo, GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini,\n    GPT-4.1, GPT-5.4. TweetEval sentiment. Measure per-subgroup misclassification\n    heterogeneity across models. Test structural persistence of heterogeneity.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    models_completed: 6\n    all_delta_pi_above_005: true\n    effective_delta_pi_range: 0.080 - 0.198\n    worst_subgroup_consensus: True_medium (5/6 models)\n    spearman_significant_pairs: 4\n    gpt35_delta_pi: 0.198\n    gpt41_delta_pi: 0.098\n    gpt4o_delta_pi: 0.08\n    gpt5x_delta_pi: 0.114\n  result_rationale: 'This experiment validates the paper''s core premise: misclassification\n    heterogeneity is STRUCTURAL, not model-specific. All 6 models across 3 generations\n    (GPT-3.5 through GPT-5.4) show Δπ > 0.05 — the threshold where the bias identity\n    predicts meaningful amplification. Best-tier reviewers see this as strong evidence\n    that the problem won''t ''go away with better models.'' The worst-subgroup consensus\n    (True_medium, 5/6 models) suggests text features driving misclassification are\n    stable across architectures. The Spearman correlations (4 significant pairs) show\n    error patterns are correlated across models — the same subgroups are hard for\n    all LLMs. This is a P1 experiment that moves to appendix but provides crucial\n    evidence for the ''this matters in practice'' argument. Best-tier reviewers cite\n    this as supporting evidence when rating practical significance.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table A9: Cross-Model Misclassification Heterogeneity (Appendix)'\n    columns:\n    - Model\n    - Overall Accuracy\n    - Effective Δπ\n    - Worst Subgroup\n    - Best Subgroup\n    rows:\n    - - GPT-3.5-turbo\n      - '0.78'\n      - '0.198'\n      - True_medium (0.39)\n      - False_medium (0.10)\n    - - GPT-4-turbo\n      - '0.83'\n      - '0.149'\n      - True_medium (0.28)\n      - False_medium (0.08)\n    - - GPT-4o\n      - '0.85'\n      - '0.080'\n      - True_medium (0.22)\n      - False_medium (0.07)\n    - - GPT-4o-mini\n      - '0.82'\n      - '0.136'\n      - True_medium (0.30)\n      - False_medium (0.09)\n    - - GPT-4.1\n      - '0.86'\n      - '0.098'\n      - True_medium (0.24)\n      - False_short (0.07)\n    - - GPT-5.4\n      - '0.84'\n      - '0.114'\n      - True_medium (0.26)\n      - False_medium (0.08)\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Appendix figure: Grouped bar chart of Δπ across 6 models, with horizontal line\n    at Δπ=0.05 threshold. All bars above threshold.'\n  analysis: 'Moved to appendix per paper framing guidelines. But best-tier reviewers\n    reference it in reviews: ''The multi-model analysis convincingly demonstrates\n    that heterogeneity is structural.'' The fact that even GPT-5.4 shows Δπ=0.114\n    preempts the ''future models will fix this'' objection.\n\n    '\n- name: Adaptive Expert Annotation Allocation\n  plan_id: exp-009\n  description: 'CATE-weighted allocation targeting high-CATE × high-error subgroups.\n    Compare against uniform and uncertainty-only allocation. Stage 3 experiment —\n    builds on exp-001 infrastructure and exp-006 budget analysis.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    allocation_strategies:\n    - uniform\n    - uncertainty_only\n    - cate_weighted\n    uniform_rmse_k4_n500: 0.12\n    uncertainty_rmse_k4_n500: 0.105\n    cate_weighted_rmse_k4_n500: 0.095\n    cate_weighted_improvement_vs_uniform: ~21%\n    cate_weighted_improvement_vs_uncertainty: ~10%\n    effective_crossover_reduction: Reduces K=4 crossover from n=1000 to ~700\n    coverage_all_strategies: 0.92-0.95\n  result_rationale: 'P2 experiment — appendix material. Under best tier, reviewers\n    see adaptive allocation as a nice-to-have extension that demonstrates the framework''s\n    practical flexibility. The 21% RMSE improvement over uniform allocation is meaningful\n    but not dramatic — it confirms that allocation matters but doesn''t change the\n    paper''s main conclusions. The ''reduces crossover from n=1000 to ~700'' result\n    tightens the budget guidance from exp-006 but is incremental. Best-tier reviewers\n    mention this favorably in ''additional contributions'' but don''t weight it heavily\n    in their overall assessment. The CATE-weighted strategy''s advantage comes from\n    concentrating expert labels where both error and effect heterogeneity are highest\n    — this is a direct application of the Δπ×Δτ diagnostic (Claim 3) to allocation,\n    creating a nice conceptual link.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table A10: Allocation Strategy Comparison (Appendix)'\n    columns:\n    - Strategy\n    - K\n    - n_expert\n    - RMSE\n    - Coverage\n    - Crossover n\n    rows:\n    - - Uniform\n      - '4'\n      - '500'\n      - '0.120'\n      - '0.93'\n      - '1000'\n    - - Uncertainty-only\n      - '4'\n      - '500'\n      - '0.105'\n      - '0.93'\n      - '850'\n    - - CATE-weighted\n      - '4'\n      - '500'\n      - '0.095'\n      - '0.94'\n      - '700'\n    - - Uniform\n      - '2'\n      - '250'\n      - '0.091'\n      - '0.94'\n      - '250'\n    - - CATE-weighted\n      - '2'\n      - '250'\n      - '0.082'\n      - '0.94'\n      - '180'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Appendix figure: Budget crossover curves under different allocation strategies,\n    showing leftward shift with CATE-weighted allocation.'\n  analysis: 'P2 experiment positioned as ''practical extension.'' Best-tier reviewers\n    note it approvingly but don''t consider it a core contribution. One reviewer may\n    suggest this as future work rather than appendix material — minor framing decision,\n    not a weakness.\n\n    '\n- name: Robustness to Misspecified Subgroup Structure\n  plan_id: exp-010\n  description: 'Test EC-HTE when subgroups are noisy/overlapping. Noise levels: 0%,\n    10%, 25%, 50% subgroup misassignment. K={2,4}. Coverage as decisive metric.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    noise_levels:\n    - 0\n    - 0.1\n    - 0.25\n    - 0.5\n    total_runs: 800\n    k2_coverage_hb_range: 0.945 - 0.958\n    k4_coverage_hb_range: 0.946 - 0.960\n    k2_coverage_naive_range: 0.875 - 0.897\n    hb_beats_mle_all_configs: true\n    graceful_convergence_at_50pct: true\n    coverage_drop_0_to_50_noise: -1.3pp (K=2), -1.0pp (K=4)\n  result_rationale: 'Robustness is essential for reviewer acceptance of Claim 2 (70-85%\n    range). The key result is graceful degradation: EC-HTE coverage drops only ~1pp\n    from 0% to 50% noise, while maintaining nominal coverage (>94.5%) at all noise\n    levels. This directly addresses reviewer concern #4 (''how sensitive to subgroup\n    definition?'') with a quantitative answer: ''not very.'' The mechanism is HB partial\n    pooling — at 50% noise, subgroups are essentially random, and HB automatically\n    shrinks toward global (its prior), recovering global correction''s behavior. This\n    is the James-Stein guarantee in action: ''never much worse than the shrinkage\n    target.'' Naive coverage (87-90%) is consistently below nominal, confirming that\n    misclassification bias — not subgroup misspecification — is the dominant source\n    of coverage failure. Best-tier reviewers see this robustness analysis as demonstrating\n    methodological maturity.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table A11: Robustness to Subgroup Noise (Appendix)'\n    columns:\n    - Noise (%)\n    - K\n    - HB Coverage\n    - Global Coverage\n    - Naive Coverage\n    - HB RMSE\n    rows:\n    - - 0%\n      - '2'\n      - '0.958'\n      - '0.925'\n      - '0.875'\n      - '0.089'\n    - - 10%\n      - '2'\n      - '0.948'\n      - '0.935'\n      - '0.877'\n      - '0.092'\n    - - 25%\n      - '2'\n      - '0.950'\n      - '0.932'\n      - '0.890'\n      - '0.094'\n    - - 50%\n      - '2'\n      - '0.945'\n      - '0.945'\n      - '0.897'\n      - '0.096'\n    - - 0%\n      - '4'\n      - '0.956'\n      - '0.930'\n      - '0.904'\n      - '0.120'\n    - - 10%\n      - '4'\n      - '0.958'\n      - '0.953'\n      - '0.916'\n      - '0.122'\n    - - 25%\n      - '4'\n      - '0.960'\n      - '0.948'\n      - '0.909'\n      - '0.125'\n    - - 50%\n      - '4'\n      - '0.946'\n      - '0.944'\n      - '0.919'\n      - '0.128'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Appendix figure: Coverage vs noise level, showing EC-HTE flat near 95% while\n    naive degrades. HB converges to global at 50% noise.'\n  analysis: 'Best-tier reviewers use this to rebut their own concern about subgroup\n    sensitivity. The ''automatic shrinkage to global at high noise'' property is intellectually\n    satisfying — it means EC-HTE is a strict generalization of global correction,\n    not a fragile alternative. Coverage as the decisive metric (not RMSE) is the right\n    framing: practitioners care about interval validity more than point estimate precision.\n\n    '\n- name: Causal Forest Integration\n  plan_id: exp-011\n  description: 'EC-HTE correction applied to CausalForestDML subgroup estimates. Compare\n    naive_forest, global_forest, ec_hte_forest. K={2,4} × regime × n_expert. 144 total\n    rows, 100 MC runs.\n\n    '\n  expected_results:\n    n_mc: 100\n    n_configs: 8\n    total_rows: 144\n    mean_abs_bias_naive_forest: 0.156\n    mean_abs_bias_global_forest: 0.107\n    mean_abs_bias_ec_hte_forest: 0.061\n    mean_rmse_naive_forest: 0.178\n    mean_rmse_global_forest: 0.166\n    mean_rmse_ec_hte_forest: 0.229\n    debiasing_hurts_max_ratio: 5.9x (K=4/extreme/sg0)\n    debiasing_hurts_cases: 9\n    k2_n500_ec_hte_bias_range: 0.004-0.013 (near zero)\n    pattern_consistent_with_dim: true\n  result_rationale: 'CausalForest integration is P0 because Claim 2 explicitly mentions\n    CausalForestDML — without this experiment, there''s a claim-evidence gap that\n    reviewers would flag as a killshot. The results show two key patterns: (1) EC-HTE\n    achieves 61% bias reduction vs naive and 43% vs global, confirming the correction\n    generalizes beyond DIM to nonlinear CATE estimators, (2) the ''debiasing hurts''\n    effect is AMPLIFIED with CausalForest (5.9× for K=4/extreme vs 2.41× for DIM)\n    — nonlinear estimation compounds the misclassification-induced bias. This amplification\n    is the headline ''5.9×'' number in the abstract. Best-tier reviewers accept the\n    RMSE tradeoff (EC-HTE RMSE 0.229 > global 0.166) because it''s disclosed honestly\n    and explained: variance-bias tradeoff from per-subgroup CM estimation. At n=500/K=2,\n    EC-HTE bias is near-zero (0.004-0.013) with competitive RMSE, showing the tradeoff\n    resolves with sufficient expert labels. The ''5.9× amplification with CausalForest''\n    result is particularly impactful for NLP audience — many CSS researchers use causal\n    forests, so this directly threatens their existing workflows.\n\n    '\n  tables:\n  - name: 'Table 8: CausalForest Integration Results (Main Paper)'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Mean |Bias|\n    - Mean RMSE\n    - Max Amplification\n    - K=2/n=500 |Bias|\n    rows:\n    - - Naive Forest\n      - '0.156'\n      - '0.178'\n      - '-'\n      - '0.125'\n    - - Global Forest\n      - '0.107'\n      - '0.166'\n      - 5.9x (K=4/extreme/sg0)\n      - 0.033-0.109\n    - - EC-HTE Forest\n      - '0.061'\n      - '0.229'\n      - '-'\n      - 0.004-0.013\n  - name: 'Table 9: Debiasing-Hurts Cases in CausalForest (Main Paper)'\n    columns:\n    - Config\n    - Subgroup\n    - Naive |Bias|\n    - Global |Bias|\n    - Amplification\n    rows:\n    - - K=4/extreme\n      - sg0 (lowest π)\n      - '0.018'\n      - '0.106'\n      - 5.9x\n    - - K=4/extreme\n      - sg1\n      - '0.045'\n      - '0.078'\n      - 1.7x\n    - - K=2/extreme\n      - sg0 (lowest π)\n      - '0.022'\n      - '0.063'\n      - 2.9x\n    - - K=2/moderate\n      - sg0\n      - '0.015'\n      - '0.031'\n      - 2.1x\n  - name: 'Table A12: Bias-RMSE Tradeoff by Budget'\n    columns:\n    - n_expert\n    - K\n    - EC-HTE |Bias|\n    - EC-HTE RMSE\n    - Global |Bias|\n    - Global RMSE\n    rows:\n    - - '100'\n      - '2'\n      - '0.025'\n      - '0.185'\n      - '0.071'\n      - '0.112'\n    - - '250'\n      - '2'\n      - '0.012'\n      - '0.128'\n      - '0.065'\n      - '0.108'\n    - - '500'\n      - '2'\n      - '0.006'\n      - '0.095'\n      - '0.059'\n      - '0.105'\n    - - '250'\n      - '4'\n      - '0.032'\n      - '0.265'\n      - '0.095'\n      - '0.145'\n    - - '500'\n      - '4'\n      - '0.018'\n      - '0.195'\n      - '0.088'\n      - '0.140'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 6 (Main): Side-by-side comparison of DIM vs CausalForest debiasing-hurts\n    effect. Shows amplification increases with nonlinear estimator, especially at\n    K=4.'\n  analysis: Best-tier reviewers see CausalForest results as the strongest evidence\n    for practical relevance. The 5.9× amplification headline number comes from this\n    experiment and is the paper's most eye-catching finding. The RMSE tradeoff is\n    disclosed in a dedicated 'Bias-RMSE Tradeoff' subsection — reviewers appreciate\n    the transparency and note that at n≥500, the tradeoff largely resolves. The pattern\n    consistency between DIM and CausalForest ('same direction, amplified magnitude')\n    strengthens the theoretical claim that the bias identity captures a fundamental\n    mechanism, not an estimator-specific artifact. Combined with exp-017 (R-learner\n    replication), this establishes estimator-agnosticism as a validated property.\n","numLines":1288,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1288}}}
