{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EHg7YtFdM2H1uKKuaUEHxG","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\\section{Conclusion}\\label{sec:conclusion}\n2\t\n3\tStandard confusion-matrix correction---the default in PPI, DSL, and CSS practice---can reverse causal conclusions when misclassification rates vary across subgroups.\n4\tWe derived an exact bias identity (Theorem~\\ref{thm:bias_identity}) explaining when and why, verified across synthetic grids, 11 LLM--dataset combinations, and demographic subgroups.\n5\tThe identity yields a diagnostic (Proposition~\\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}) that flags harmful correction from error rates alone, and prescribes per-subgroup correction that eliminates sign reversal, given sufficient error heterogeneity and expert budget."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01N3dJcrSJw8fGd5EAvrcF1g","session_id":"5a7fea04-88c6-4f40-a7d8-de32a19d47e7","uuid":"56b1a02a-928d-46e0-8634-7554762597e5","timestamp":"2026-05-23T20:13:09.124Z"}
